WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.420
Narrator: From wading in the 9/11 
Memorial pools

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.713
 in New York City

00:00:04.713 --> 00:00:06.464
 and removing dangerous rocks 

00:00:06.464 --> 00:00:08.633
halfway up the Niagara Falls cliffs,

00:00:08.717 --> 00:00:12.971
to sanitizing the train cars of Tokyo's 
renowned metro system, 

00:00:13.221 --> 00:00:16.975
here's everything we deep cleaned in 2023.

00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:23.523
Narrator: This is Jim.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:25.442
He's cleaning the reflecting pools

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:28.570
at the 9/11 Memorial in New York City.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.613
Jim and his team are working beneath

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:35.035
a 27-foot-tall waterfall
in near-total darkness,

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:37.412
vacuuming every inch of the pool.

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:40.874
Their goal is to do it
without missing a spot.

00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:43.043
It's a physically demanding job,

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:45.587
but that's not the only challenge.

00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:48.006
Jim: I've never actually
marked how many miles it is,

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:49.883
but you do a few miles in there,

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:52.677
and it could be humid,
and you come out of there

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:56.097
and you feel like you
walked on the moon, almost.

00:00:57.182 --> 00:00:59.642
Narrator: The pools are
nearly an acre each,

00:00:59.642 --> 00:01:01.728
so it will take hours to finish the job

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.396
before the sun comes up.

00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:06.357
While he works inside the pool,

00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:09.486
other crew members work on
the bronze panels aboveground.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.282
These routines happen five
days a week and are essential

00:01:14.282 --> 00:01:17.160
for keeping the memorial
in pristine condition

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.080
for the millions who come
to pay their respects here.

00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:27.212
They start by shutting off the waterfalls,

00:01:27.212 --> 00:01:28.797
and over the next eight hours,

00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:32.467
Jim and his team work tirelessly
to deep clean this pool.

00:01:37.722 --> 00:01:40.517
Jim: The hardest part is it's a long task.

00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:44.687
The pool is big. It's not a swimming pool.

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:47.857
It's a 200-by-200 giant pool.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.153
I'm going to pull the hose
out and then bring it back.

00:01:52.153 --> 00:01:54.239
Rian: Gotcha.

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:56.074
Jim: You don't have to use the whole reel.

00:01:56.074 --> 00:01:58.034
We got too much hose.

00:01:58.034 --> 00:02:00.870
We usually have three
men through the pool,

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.373
so two guys will be in the pool

00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:07.293
and one guy will be
outside running the pumps,

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:09.629
which collect the debris

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.966
and return the clean
water back to the pool.

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:15.343
Once we're set, we're on our way.

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:17.971
I go and vacuum, generally,

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:21.808
and whoever's with me
that night brushes behind.

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:22.892
Come on, hurry up.

00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:25.228
Rian: Sorry.

00:02:25.228 --> 00:02:26.646
Jim: We don't got all day here.

00:02:28.022 --> 00:02:31.109
The vacuum will capture
most of the debris,

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:34.529
and the person brushing will knock up

00:02:34.529 --> 00:02:37.532
some of the other loose debris
that doesn't get caught,

00:02:37.532 --> 00:02:40.076
and it will be caught
into some of our filters

00:02:40.076 --> 00:02:42.078
that we have running downstairs.

00:02:43.371 --> 00:02:45.623
Now I'm trapped in the hose here.

00:02:48.418 --> 00:02:50.170
I'm going to trip myself.

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:53.381
And we'll be on "America's Bloopers."

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.552
Narrator: The workers generally
clean out dirt, leaves,

00:02:57.552 --> 00:02:59.971
and algae from the bottom of the pools.

00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:01.973
They also remove larger items

00:03:01.973 --> 00:03:04.350
that visitors drop or throw in.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.560
They don't always know

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.770
what they'll encounter
in the pool each night.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.233
Jim: We find kids' toys,
little small items.

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:14.068
Depends on the crowd.

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:17.488
One night I actually found a bat in there.

00:03:17.488 --> 00:03:19.032
And I'm not talking about a baseball bat,

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:20.533
I'm talking about a bat.

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.577
It was pretty wild when
he flew away from me

00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:25.580
when I realized I was grabbing a bat.

00:03:26.414 --> 00:03:29.083
Depending on the cleanliness of the pool,

00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:31.252
it could take anywhere between

00:03:31.252 --> 00:03:34.172
six and seven hours.

00:03:34.172 --> 00:03:36.966
And sometimes, if we feel up to it,

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:39.260
we could do it a little quicker.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.222
Some people treat this
place like a wishing well.

00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:44.057
So we do catch some money,

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.309
and unfortunately I think some people,

00:03:46.309 --> 00:03:50.146
being that it's so far removed
from the original 9/11,

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:52.023
I think some people are a little

00:03:52.023 --> 00:03:54.108
uneducated about this place,

00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:56.694
and some people treat it
like just a regular fountain,

00:03:56.694 --> 00:03:59.572
and they're throwing garbage
in here sometimes too.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:04.577
People have to learn that
this isn't your mall fountain.

00:04:04.994 --> 00:04:09.249
I knew a few of the
brokers that passed away,

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:12.835
a whole group of them who had a meeting

00:04:12.835 --> 00:04:16.130
in one of the towers, unfortunately, and,

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.551
except for one trader,
all of them perished.

00:04:20.551 --> 00:04:23.304
I think about them often

00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:26.057
because we go up top where the names are

00:04:26.057 --> 00:04:28.977
and brush near the
nameplates, the troughs,

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:33.606
and on the north side,
they're all on one panel.

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:37.235
So I see their names just about every day.

00:04:37.235 --> 00:04:38.987
Total waste of lives

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:43.116
that could have been doing
good in the world now.

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:44.200
Narrator: While Jim and the team

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.327
finish cleaning the pools below,

00:04:46.327 --> 00:04:50.540
Ryan is tackling the
bronze name panels above.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:55.211
The panels honor the 2,983
victims of the attacks

00:04:55.211 --> 00:05:00.216
on the World Trade Center,
both in 1993 and 2001.

00:05:00.258 --> 00:05:02.969
Ryan will have to strip
the top layer of metal

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:05.346
and use a blowtorch to remove a carving

00:05:05.346 --> 00:05:07.390
someone etched onto the plaque,

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:10.351
without damaging the rest of the memorial.

00:05:11.519 --> 00:05:14.439
Ryan: Confuses me why you'd
want to write something here.

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:21.696
For a mark like that,

00:05:21.696 --> 00:05:24.949
you have to go pretty
deep, get all the way down.

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:26.909
Otherwise you can't really get rid of it.

00:05:26.909 --> 00:05:28.953
If you don't go down far enough,

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:30.997
you'll see it when you refinish it.

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:33.458
The further down you go, the better.

00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:35.376
Sometimes I've seen nice things written,

00:05:35.376 --> 00:05:37.879
but, you know, they don't understand.

00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:40.089
You can't just write
whatever you want on it.

00:05:40.965 --> 00:05:43.051
Narrator: After briefly
assessing the damage,

00:05:43.051 --> 00:05:45.720
Ryan needs to remove the protective bars

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.598
so he can reach the entire metal surface.

00:05:48.598 --> 00:05:51.726
To do this, he props up
the bars with a wood plank

00:05:51.726 --> 00:05:54.354
and unhooks the brackets
holding up the panels.

00:05:56.064 --> 00:05:57.607
Ryan: Whew!

00:05:57.607 --> 00:06:00.651
Now what I'll do is rub
it down with the naphtha.

00:06:00.651 --> 00:06:02.278
It's almost like a paint thinner.

00:06:02.278 --> 00:06:05.365
It just kind of takes the wax
off, so I'm not burning wax.

00:06:07.408 --> 00:06:08.910
If I can open it.

00:06:11.662 --> 00:06:14.165
And it shouldn't take too long
once I get the torch on it.

00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:16.626
There's so much in the prep,

00:06:16.626 --> 00:06:18.002
and then the finishing,

00:06:18.002 --> 00:06:21.839
the torch is actually not that bad.

00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:24.384
Narrator: After sanding
down the top layer of panel

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:26.219
and neutralizing the wax,

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:29.555
Ryan is ready to
blowtorch the damaged area

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.349
to melt the metal in the patina

00:06:31.349 --> 00:06:33.267
so he can repaint the color layer

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:35.603
and restore the panel
to its original state.

00:06:38.272 --> 00:06:39.982
Ryan: This is what we use,

00:06:41.442 --> 00:06:43.820
and it's basically just

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.822
a patina in a solution.

00:06:47.156 --> 00:06:49.409
Ah, I haven't used you in a while.

00:06:52.787 --> 00:06:54.163
Prep them.

00:06:58.626 --> 00:07:01.379
So, I'm basically getting
up to the right temperature.

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:04.215
If it's too cold, it'll go on spotty.

00:07:04.215 --> 00:07:06.551
If it's too hot, it'll
kind of evaporate off

00:07:06.551 --> 00:07:08.094
or it'll get burnt.

00:07:09.512 --> 00:07:11.931
Narrator: In the summer months,
the longest part of the job

00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:14.183
is waiting for the burned panel to cool

00:07:14.183 --> 00:07:18.187
before applying a new layer
of wax and an acrylic topcoat

00:07:18.187 --> 00:07:20.189
to finish the restoration process.

00:07:21.357 --> 00:07:23.860
We're going to put the
acrylic spray on it,

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:27.905
just kind of like a clear coat.

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:30.241
It helps it kind of adhere
a little bit better.

00:07:31.742 --> 00:07:34.704
The last step, really. Well,
besides buffing the wax off.

00:07:34.704 --> 00:07:36.372
So I'm just going to put
the wax on real nice.

00:07:36.372 --> 00:07:40.168
Not too hard, not a lot.
You don't need a ton.

00:07:40.168 --> 00:07:41.919
Just put it on lightly.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.716
The wax will go over the
finish and make it look,

00:07:46.716 --> 00:07:50.386
once I polish it, it'll look brand new.

00:07:50.386 --> 00:07:51.387
We got a little bit of heat,

00:07:51.387 --> 00:07:53.681
but that's because it's still hot.

00:07:56.684 --> 00:07:59.103
But it was right here.

00:07:59.937 --> 00:08:03.691
Narrator: Now all Ryan has to
do is buff off the excess wax

00:08:03.691 --> 00:08:06.152
using a white Scotch-Brite pad.

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:09.113
Ryan: And then once it gets
smooth, you know you're done.

00:08:10.114 --> 00:08:11.908
When people do something like that,

00:08:11.908 --> 00:08:14.660
I wouldn't say it makes
me angry. I get it.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.621
Some of these people might
not have even been alive

00:08:16.621 --> 00:08:19.081
when it happened, or they
were too young to get it.

00:08:19.081 --> 00:08:22.210
My uncle was a New York City firefighter,

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:23.878
and he was hurt really bad here,

00:08:23.878 --> 00:08:27.965
but luckily he survived.
We got a little lucky.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.470
Narrator: After a strenuous
six to seven hours of cleaning,

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:35.139
Jim and the team are ready
to head out of the pool.

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:38.768
By tomorrow morning, a
new batch of visitors

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:42.605
will see sparkling pools
below rushing waterfalls,

00:08:42.605 --> 00:08:47.443
and most of them won't know
that Jim's team was even here.

00:08:47.443 --> 00:08:51.656
Jim: When we finish the
pool, most of the time,

00:08:51.656 --> 00:08:53.324
we're just ready to
really get out of there.

00:08:53.324 --> 00:08:56.202
And most of our inspection,

00:08:56.202 --> 00:08:58.955
we're fairly confident
that we did the proper job.

00:08:58.955 --> 00:09:01.165
There's always a little fail factor,

00:09:01.165 --> 00:09:04.168
but not too bad.

00:09:07.505 --> 00:09:12.301
My father worked in these
buildings almost his whole life.

00:09:12.301 --> 00:09:16.430
And my father was here
for the '93 bombing,

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:18.849
and that was before
cellphones and everything,

00:09:18.849 --> 00:09:23.521
and he was on the 30th
floor of the second tower.

00:09:23.521 --> 00:09:26.190
When he was there, he
was the floor marshal.

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.568
He came home full of
soot, but he stayed there

00:09:28.568 --> 00:09:30.695
because there was people that
couldn't get down the steps.

00:09:30.695 --> 00:09:33.406
So he made sure that he stayed with them.

00:09:33.406 --> 00:09:37.410
So I think on 9/11 day, if he was here,

00:09:38.369 --> 00:09:42.039
we probably would've lost him
because he wouldn't have left.

00:09:42.039 --> 00:09:44.417
He passed away a couple years ago.

00:09:45.334 --> 00:09:47.878
He was always so proud of the job.

00:09:47.878 --> 00:09:51.382
I probably had chances to do
other things in this building,

00:09:51.382 --> 00:09:54.719
but even if I wanted to do another job,

00:09:54.719 --> 00:09:56.095
I wouldn't, because of him.

00:09:56.095 --> 00:10:00.433
And now, I love doing it, so I do it.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.231
Narrator: Next up, we travel to Japan

00:10:07.231 --> 00:10:10.568
 to see how the Tokyo Metro keeps its reputation

00:10:10.568 --> 00:10:14.030
as one of the cleanest transportation 
systems in the world. 

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:17.950
Narrator: This is Takayuki,

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:20.995
a cleaning supervisor at
the rail yard in Ayase.

00:10:20.995 --> 00:10:24.498
He's worked for Tokyo
Metro for almost 41 years,

00:10:24.498 --> 00:10:26.167
and it's his job to make sure

00:10:26.167 --> 00:10:28.419
the trains are cleaned thoroughly.

00:10:40.556 --> 00:10:42.516
Narrator: The Tokyo Metro system is famous

00:10:42.516 --> 00:10:45.770
for being one of the cleanest
subway systems in the world.

00:10:46.771 --> 00:10:50.691
It transports an average of
about 6 million people daily,

00:10:50.691 --> 00:10:52.485
and maintaining that reputation

00:10:52.485 --> 00:10:56.489
takes both an extensive amount
of operational organization

00:10:56.489 --> 00:10:58.824
and a ton of manpower.

00:10:58.824 --> 00:11:01.160
The cleaning staff's
approach is straightforward

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:04.038
but uncompromising: whatever Tokyo Metro's

00:11:04.038 --> 00:11:08.376
millions of daily passengers
touch must be cleaned.

00:11:08.376 --> 00:11:10.336
Today, they're cleaning the train cars

00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:14.090
at the Ayase rail yard,
which they do every 15 days.

00:11:14.965 --> 00:11:17.051
The cleaners take careful measures

00:11:17.051 --> 00:11:19.720
to ensure each nook and cranny is cleaned.

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:22.640
Then we'll head to the
Ginza-itchome Station,

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.017
located in one of the
busiest shopping areas

00:11:25.017 --> 00:11:26.435
in Tokyo, Japan,

00:11:26.435 --> 00:11:28.896
where they clean the
station top to bottom.

00:11:30.189 --> 00:11:33.901
There are 41 trains on the
Tokyo Metro Chiyoda Line.

00:11:33.901 --> 00:11:36.696
The team cleans three
to four trains a day.

00:11:37.613 --> 00:11:39.657
Once a train comes into the rail yard,

00:11:39.657 --> 00:11:42.368
cleaners start by picking
up any trash they see.

00:11:43.577 --> 00:11:46.372
It takes four workers
to clean the exterior.

00:11:46.372 --> 00:11:48.958
It's all done by hand, and cleaners start

00:11:48.958 --> 00:11:51.210
by hosing it down with water

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:53.963
and then using brushes
to scrub it with soap.

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:31.751
Narrator: If the stain does not come off

00:12:31.751 --> 00:12:32.960
with a brush and soap,

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:34.920
then they use a pad to scrub it off.

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:39.383
Next, they rinse off all the soap.

00:12:40.593 --> 00:12:43.679
Meanwhile, other staffers
clean the interior.

00:12:43.679 --> 00:12:47.099
It takes 10 workers, and they
start at the top of the car

00:12:47.099 --> 00:12:49.435
and work their way down to the bottom.

00:13:15.878 --> 00:13:18.297
Narrator: The first step
is to clean the area

00:13:18.297 --> 00:13:20.841
where advertisements are placed.

00:13:20.841 --> 00:13:24.303
Then they clean the luggage
racks, and then the wall area.

00:13:24.303 --> 00:13:27.223
They use rags and dusters
to clean each part.

00:13:38.692 --> 00:13:40.319
Narrator: Cleaners then tackle the floors

00:13:40.319 --> 00:13:42.196
and start by mopping.

00:13:42.196 --> 00:13:44.698
They also use sticks to clean crevices

00:13:44.698 --> 00:13:46.575
near the entrance of each door.

00:13:48.118 --> 00:13:51.914
Twice a year they wax the
floors of each train car.

00:13:51.914 --> 00:13:54.416
They remove all the old
wax with a polisher,

00:13:54.416 --> 00:13:56.502
and then they let it dry.

00:13:56.502 --> 00:14:00.130
Once it's dry, they
apply two coats of wax.

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:01.841
It takes about an hour and a half

00:14:01.841 --> 00:14:03.300
from the time the wax is removed

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.094
to the time the new wax dries.

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:24.238
Narrator: Altogether, it takes workers

00:14:24.238 --> 00:14:25.364
a little over an hour

00:14:25.364 --> 00:14:28.117
to clean the trains inside and out.

00:14:28.117 --> 00:14:29.827
Once the cleaning is complete,

00:14:29.827 --> 00:14:31.996
Takayuki begins his inspection.

00:15:16.749 --> 00:15:18.751
Narrator: Once Takayuki
approves the cleaning,

00:15:18.751 --> 00:15:20.252
the trains return to service,

00:15:20.252 --> 00:15:24.465
ready to transport millions
of passengers all over again.

00:15:26.133 --> 00:15:28.552
Inside the Ginza-itchome Station,

00:15:28.552 --> 00:15:32.306
Chikako and Kenji work to
clean the station daily.

00:15:55.245 --> 00:15:57.623
Narrator: Kazuhiro has been
working for Tokyo Metro

00:15:57.623 --> 00:15:59.375
since he was 18.

00:15:59.375 --> 00:16:01.335
He got his train license at 20

00:16:01.335 --> 00:16:04.380
and drove trains until he was 57.

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:07.383
He leads the team responsible
for the cleaning and security

00:16:07.383 --> 00:16:09.718
for this station and a few others.

00:16:09.718 --> 00:16:11.845
There are nine Tokyo Metro train lines

00:16:11.845 --> 00:16:14.515
and 180 stations that provide service

00:16:14.515 --> 00:16:16.475
to the greater Tokyo area.

00:16:45.546 --> 00:16:46.797
Narrator: The escalator handrails

00:16:46.797 --> 00:16:49.258
are a high-touch surface
within the station,

00:16:49.258 --> 00:16:51.927
and Chikako takes extra
care to wipe the belt

00:16:51.927 --> 00:16:53.303
with wet washcloths.

00:16:53.303 --> 00:16:55.472
She rides the escalators up and down

00:16:55.472 --> 00:16:57.307
until they are all clean.

00:17:25.502 --> 00:17:27.129
Narrator: Chikako is also responsible

00:17:27.129 --> 00:17:29.214
for the dirtiest job of the day,

00:17:29.214 --> 00:17:32.634
cleaning the two restrooms
in the Ginza-itchome Station.

00:17:32.634 --> 00:17:34.803
The main cleanings happen once a day.

00:17:34.803 --> 00:17:38.140
She has been doing this
work for the past 10 years.

00:18:04.416 --> 00:18:06.251
Narrator: The first thing Chikako does

00:18:06.251 --> 00:18:09.129
is check for any large pieces of trash.

00:18:09.129 --> 00:18:11.298
If there are none, she sets up a fan

00:18:11.298 --> 00:18:14.051
and leaves it on throughout
the cleaning process.

00:18:14.051 --> 00:18:16.678
Efficiency is one of the
key parts of her job,

00:18:16.678 --> 00:18:18.889
and the fan allows the
bathroom to dry quickly,

00:18:18.889 --> 00:18:22.184
so as not to disrupt any
customers who may need to use it.

00:18:49.169 --> 00:18:51.088
Narrator: Chikako sticks
the ozone-machine hose

00:18:51.088 --> 00:18:53.465
down the toilet and turns it on.

00:18:54.758 --> 00:18:57.219
Then she scrubs each toilet by hand

00:18:57.219 --> 00:18:59.680
to make sure it's clean
for the next customer.

00:19:01.557 --> 00:19:03.475
She also cleans the walls.

00:19:03.475 --> 00:19:05.727
It's important that she's
using the right tools

00:19:05.727 --> 00:19:09.690
for each space, so as not
to cross-contaminate areas.

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:11.608
She uses a red dust cloth to clean

00:19:11.608 --> 00:19:13.235
areas from the knees down,

00:19:13.235 --> 00:19:15.863
which is called the "contamination zone,"

00:19:15.863 --> 00:19:17.865
and a green one for "clean zones,"

00:19:17.865 --> 00:19:21.702
which are areas above the knees,
such as mirrors and sinks.

00:20:12.211 --> 00:20:14.338
Narrator: Chikako is also
responsible for cleaning

00:20:14.338 --> 00:20:18.133
the barrier between the
platform and the train doors.

00:20:18.133 --> 00:20:19.885
The top part is cleaned daily,

00:20:19.885 --> 00:20:22.888
while the lower part
is cleaned once a week.

00:20:22.888 --> 00:20:25.682
The staff makes sure the
station platforms and walkways

00:20:25.682 --> 00:20:27.017
are clean for customers.

00:20:28.435 --> 00:20:30.896
They do cleanings with brooms every day,

00:20:30.896 --> 00:20:32.981
but they also use a
machine to clean the floors

00:20:32.981 --> 00:20:34.608
twice a month.

00:20:34.608 --> 00:20:37.611
The machine works by sprinkling
water onto the floor,

00:20:37.611 --> 00:20:39.029
scrubbing with a brush,

00:20:39.029 --> 00:20:42.741
and then collecting the
dirty water with a squeegee.

00:20:42.741 --> 00:20:45.202
This way, there is no water left behind

00:20:45.202 --> 00:20:46.954
and no slippery floors.

00:20:53.919 --> 00:20:55.671
Narrator: Kenji also goes around daily

00:20:55.671 --> 00:20:58.548
with a shoulder vacuum
and manually vacuums up

00:20:58.548 --> 00:21:00.634
any trash and dust he comes across,

00:21:00.634 --> 00:21:02.844
especially in hard-to-reach areas.

00:21:22.364 --> 00:21:24.616
Narrator: Japan has a
long history with cleaning

00:21:24.616 --> 00:21:27.619
and cleanliness that persists today.

00:22:22.090 --> 00:22:23.925
Narrator: This is Rivers.

00:22:23.925 --> 00:22:26.511
He's wading through a pool
of waste beneath one of

00:22:26.511 --> 00:22:30.140
the largest aquariums in
the world, Georgia Aquarium.

00:22:31.391 --> 00:22:35.812
It's a filthy job, but that's
not the only challenge.

00:22:35.812 --> 00:22:38.273
There's a lot of waste to manage.

00:22:38.273 --> 00:22:39.649
When this basin is full,

00:22:39.649 --> 00:22:43.653
it can hold between 5,000
to 10,000 gallons of poop

00:22:43.653 --> 00:22:46.615
that funnels here from
the aquarium's habitats.

00:22:46.615 --> 00:22:49.868
And it's the culmination of
many routine cleaning procedures

00:22:49.868 --> 00:22:52.662
that happen at the aquarium each week,

00:22:52.662 --> 00:22:54.206
without which the safety

00:22:54.206 --> 00:22:56.541
and health of the
animals would be at risk.

00:22:57.501 --> 00:23:00.462
By the time Rivers and his
team's work here is complete,

00:23:00.462 --> 00:23:04.091
the wastewater from this basin
will be cleaned, filtered,

00:23:04.091 --> 00:23:06.134
and reused as top-off water

00:23:06.134 --> 00:23:10.222
for the aquarium's largest
exhibit, Ocean Voyager,

00:23:10.222 --> 00:23:12.891
and the cleaning cycle will begin again.

00:23:12.891 --> 00:23:14.976
But before Rivers can do his job,

00:23:14.976 --> 00:23:18.688
several other critical
procedures have to happen first.

00:23:18.688 --> 00:23:21.274
Every day, specialized teams gear up

00:23:21.274 --> 00:23:23.902
to clean the hundreds of
exhibits at the aquarium.

00:23:25.695 --> 00:23:28.365
One of the first you'll see
when you enter the aquarium

00:23:28.365 --> 00:23:29.991
is the sea lion exhibit,

00:23:29.991 --> 00:23:34.955
which is home to this guy,
Toby, a 279-pound harbor seal.

00:23:35.539 --> 00:23:38.583
Before a team of divers can
begin cleaning his habitat,

00:23:38.583 --> 00:23:41.962
Toby and the other sea lions
need to vacate their pools.

00:23:41.962 --> 00:23:44.673
Staffers use this time to
do their weekly weigh-ins.

00:23:44.673 --> 00:23:46.758
Trainer: And if we're
talking about dirty jobs,

00:23:46.758 --> 00:23:49.594
he just urinated on the scale,
and now we've got to clean it.

00:23:49.594 --> 00:23:50.762
Side effect of the job.

00:23:52.431 --> 00:23:54.433
Alex: This pool, it's all sea lion poop.

00:23:54.433 --> 00:23:55.350
It's all sea lion poop.

00:23:55.350 --> 00:23:56.935
Everything that she's vacuuming from here

00:23:56.935 --> 00:23:58.770
is going into that waste basin.

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:01.982
So whatever we do in here,
the LSS, or life-support team,

00:24:01.982 --> 00:24:04.401
then has to clean up from their end.

00:24:04.401 --> 00:24:06.570
Narrator: Megan Wade
leads the pinnipeds team

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:09.656
to clean the decks and assist
the dive operations team

00:24:09.656 --> 00:24:12.075
as they clean the pools
before guests arrive.

00:24:15.454 --> 00:24:20.083
This space is home to 14 sea
lions and three harbor seals.

00:24:20.083 --> 00:24:24.463
The sea lions can eat 5%
to 8% of their body weight,

00:24:24.463 --> 00:24:25.964
making the sea lion exhibit

00:24:25.964 --> 00:24:28.550
one of the dirtiest in the aquarium.

00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:31.052
Megan: Every single morning
we come in first thing

00:24:31.052 --> 00:24:34.723
and prepare their food, and
this is restaurant-quality fish.

00:24:34.723 --> 00:24:36.683
So they get numerous species of fish,

00:24:36.683 --> 00:24:38.477
about five different species.

00:24:38.477 --> 00:24:41.146
And every single morning,
one or two people

00:24:41.146 --> 00:24:44.441
will come in and inspect
every single fish.

00:24:44.441 --> 00:24:46.818
So we're looking for missing eyes,

00:24:46.818 --> 00:24:50.155
missing fins, gills, scrapes, cuts.

00:24:50.155 --> 00:24:53.074
Anywhere where you're going to
see that can breed bacteria.

00:24:53.074 --> 00:24:55.660
So that fish automatically
gets sorted out,

00:24:55.660 --> 00:24:56.912
and we don't use it at all.

00:24:56.912 --> 00:24:59.122
We weigh out their food into buckets.

00:24:59.122 --> 00:25:01.124
And then we'll look at it again

00:25:01.124 --> 00:25:04.252
one last time before
feeding it to the animals.

00:25:04.252 --> 00:25:07.255
Once a week, every Wednesday,
it's weigh-day Wednesday.

00:25:07.255 --> 00:25:08.590
Follow me this way.

00:25:08.590 --> 00:25:10.467
We have our blue scale right here.

00:25:10.467 --> 00:25:12.135
And then where the green is lit up

00:25:12.135 --> 00:25:14.179
is going to tell us how much they weigh.

00:25:14.179 --> 00:25:15.764
So each sea lion will walk

00:25:15.764 --> 00:25:18.266
from their habitat down this hallway.

00:25:18.266 --> 00:25:19.684
We'll get their weight,

00:25:19.684 --> 00:25:21.937
and then they'll walk
back to their habitat.

00:25:22.979 --> 00:25:24.731
Narrator: After the sea lions are fed,

00:25:24.731 --> 00:25:27.442
weighed, and out of the main spaces,

00:25:27.442 --> 00:25:31.696
Megan and the team start working
on cleaning the deck areas.

00:25:31.696 --> 00:25:34.282
Her team splits up between the back pools

00:25:34.282 --> 00:25:35.325
and the main gallery,

00:25:35.325 --> 00:25:38.870
where they detail every nook and cranny.

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:40.622
As soon as you come in in the morning,

00:25:40.622 --> 00:25:43.083
you can usually smell

00:25:43.083 --> 00:25:44.751
that they have been sleeping overnight.

00:25:44.751 --> 00:25:46.294
We have high-up platforms

00:25:46.294 --> 00:25:48.547
that they like to sleep on, the rocks.

00:25:48.547 --> 00:25:50.090
And when you're sleeping,

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:53.093
and you don't get up
to go to the bathroom,

00:25:53.093 --> 00:25:54.427
it all just sits there.

00:25:54.427 --> 00:25:56.721
So if we didn't clean every single day,

00:25:56.721 --> 00:26:00.517
it would be really stinky,
kind of an ammonia smell.

00:26:00.517 --> 00:26:03.687
Also, there's fecal matter as well.

00:26:03.687 --> 00:26:06.314
We are going to detail this whole area.

00:26:06.314 --> 00:26:08.984
It gets cleaned every single day,

00:26:08.984 --> 00:26:12.112
but we detail it once a week, every week.

00:26:12.112 --> 00:26:14.781
Three male sea lions
and one male harbor seal

00:26:14.781 --> 00:26:16.950
all slept out here last night.

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:20.161
So it's probably going to be pretty messy.

00:26:20.161 --> 00:26:22.872
The chemicals that we use
are to really break up

00:26:22.872 --> 00:26:26.418
that oil that's on the surface
that the sea lions secrete.

00:26:26.418 --> 00:26:27.919
So it's just soap and water.

00:26:27.919 --> 00:26:30.380
And then we'll use some chlorhexidine

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:32.757
to disinfect the area afterwards.

00:26:33.675 --> 00:26:38.388
And then down here, Gail has
like a nice little hand brush,

00:26:38.388 --> 00:26:41.891
and she's actually doing
one of the dirtiest jobs.

00:26:41.891 --> 00:26:44.060
She's cleaning the drains.

00:26:44.060 --> 00:26:47.689
All of this is going to
always be hosed to a drain

00:26:47.689 --> 00:26:50.317
away from the water the sea lions swim in.

00:26:50.317 --> 00:26:53.486
She's taking the grates off,
cleaning inside the drain,

00:26:53.486 --> 00:26:57.240
so that everything flows
nicely each day that we clean.

00:27:00.118 --> 00:27:02.871
So the drains are part of the detailing.

00:27:02.871 --> 00:27:04.664
The removal of the net

00:27:04.664 --> 00:27:06.916
and soaking, that's part of the detailing.

00:27:06.916 --> 00:27:08.460
Sea lions, when they breathe,

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:10.754
sometimes shoot snot out of their nose.

00:27:10.754 --> 00:27:12.881
So a lot of times in sea lion habitats,

00:27:12.881 --> 00:27:15.342
you'll see brown snot on the walls.

00:27:15.342 --> 00:27:17.761
So we'll need to scrub that off, too.

00:27:17.761 --> 00:27:19.679
My favorite part about cleaning the deck

00:27:19.679 --> 00:27:23.016
is getting to net the
poop out of the water,

00:27:23.016 --> 00:27:27.771
because it's just so reinforcing
when you do a good job

00:27:27.771 --> 00:27:30.565
and you get good at it.
So I just love doing it.

00:27:32.692 --> 00:27:34.319
Narrator: After Megan
and the pinnipeds team

00:27:34.319 --> 00:27:36.237
work on the main gallery deck,

00:27:36.237 --> 00:27:38.281
Alex Glick and a team of divers

00:27:38.281 --> 00:27:41.660
work on everything below
deck at the sea lion exhibit.

00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:43.370
Alex: Today you're going
to start in pool two

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:44.954
and then do pool three.

00:27:44.954 --> 00:27:48.083
This one's vacuuming and floor-scrubbing.

00:27:48.083 --> 00:27:49.959
And then pool three, you'll
be vacuuming rockwork

00:27:49.959 --> 00:27:51.503
and then a final floor scrub.

00:27:51.503 --> 00:27:54.047
So we do this dive on just normal scuba.

00:27:54.047 --> 00:27:56.383
Even though it's only an 8-foot pool,

00:27:56.383 --> 00:27:57.467
we still do scuba diving

00:27:57.467 --> 00:27:59.344
so that you have freedom of movement.

00:28:01.554 --> 00:28:03.014
This is our vacuum hose.

00:28:03.014 --> 00:28:04.849
They actually plug this in in the water,

00:28:04.849 --> 00:28:07.477
and we have to make a radio
call to our life support system,

00:28:07.477 --> 00:28:09.354
and they'll turn the vacuum on.

00:28:09.354 --> 00:28:11.690
So we drop the vacuum head in the water

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:14.275
with the hose attached to the diver.

00:28:14.275 --> 00:28:15.652
And then they'll prime the hose

00:28:15.652 --> 00:28:17.779
to get all of the air out of the line.

00:28:17.779 --> 00:28:20.573
This pool, it's all sea lion poop.

00:28:20.573 --> 00:28:23.535
You want to get as much vacuumed up

00:28:23.535 --> 00:28:25.912
before you start scrubbing the floor.

00:28:25.912 --> 00:28:26.996
Narrator: This scrubber is one of

00:28:26.996 --> 00:28:28.873
the key tools the divers use

00:28:28.873 --> 00:28:32.127
to thoroughly clean
everything under the water.

00:28:32.127 --> 00:28:34.003
A hose pumps water through the engine,

00:28:34.003 --> 00:28:35.505
which powers the scrubber.

00:28:35.505 --> 00:28:37.882
This high-pressure water
makes cleaning the floors

00:28:37.882 --> 00:28:39.801
and walls much easier.

00:28:40.719 --> 00:28:42.762
The single scrubber has hard bristles

00:28:42.762 --> 00:28:45.807
and is used for rockwork,
while the double-brush scrubber

00:28:45.807 --> 00:28:48.560
is used to clean the walls
and painted surfaces.

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:50.687
Alex: Once she's done vacuuming,
I'll give this to her,

00:28:50.687 --> 00:28:52.272
and she will then clean the floor.

00:28:52.272 --> 00:28:53.481
And she's holding the trigger down,

00:28:53.481 --> 00:28:54.983
and she's just swimming back and forth,

00:28:54.983 --> 00:28:57.819
doing kind of like a lawnmower motion,

00:28:57.819 --> 00:28:59.404
cleaning one side to the next

00:28:59.404 --> 00:29:01.239
to get every single inch of the pool.

00:29:02.699 --> 00:29:04.409
If she comes across an area
that's particularly dirty,

00:29:04.409 --> 00:29:06.828
she might slow down and
spend a little time on it

00:29:06.828 --> 00:29:09.748
to get up whatever scum is on
the bottom and then move on.

00:29:11.249 --> 00:29:13.126
Narrator: Animal debris
doesn't just settle

00:29:13.126 --> 00:29:15.253
on the habitat's floors and surfaces.

00:29:15.253 --> 00:29:18.590
It also collects in
grates and skimmer boxes.

00:29:18.590 --> 00:29:22.427
These outflows help manage
waste between routine cleanings

00:29:22.427 --> 00:29:25.513
by separating animals
from some of the debris.

00:29:25.513 --> 00:29:28.933
In total, it takes Alex
and his team of divers

00:29:28.933 --> 00:29:32.604
roughly an hour to complete
their work in both pools.

00:29:33.688 --> 00:29:37.192
On the other side of the
aquarium is Ocean Voyager,

00:29:37.192 --> 00:29:41.780
the largest indoor aquatic
exhibit in the United States.

00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:46.201
It holds 6.3 million gallons of water

00:29:46.201 --> 00:29:49.120
and is home to whale sharks, manta rays,

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:51.164
and thousands of other fish.

00:29:51.164 --> 00:29:53.625
This space is largely dependent

00:29:53.625 --> 00:29:57.045
on an advanced life-support
and filtration system

00:29:57.045 --> 00:29:59.172
but still relies on teams

00:29:59.172 --> 00:30:01.883
for maintenance and hands-on cleaning.

00:30:01.883 --> 00:30:05.386
Courtney: In Ocean Voyager,
we have two whale sharks.

00:30:05.386 --> 00:30:06.763
There's Yushan, which is
the larger whale shark,

00:30:06.763 --> 00:30:08.097
and then there's Taroko.

00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:13.061
Common misconception about whale sharks

00:30:13.061 --> 00:30:14.813
would be that they're dangerous.

00:30:14.813 --> 00:30:17.899
The most intimidating
thing is they're just big.

00:30:17.899 --> 00:30:18.733
They just are big.

00:30:18.733 --> 00:30:21.653
And so if you're in the way,
you've just got to move.

00:30:21.653 --> 00:30:23.196
They are not out to hurt you.

00:30:23.196 --> 00:30:26.241
Like, their throat is only
a the size of a quarter.

00:30:26.241 --> 00:30:28.284
They're very, very docile creatures,

00:30:28.284 --> 00:30:30.745
and they just want to cruise along.

00:30:31.788 --> 00:30:35.500
We usually have four to eight divers

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:37.669
in our everyday maintenance dive.

00:30:37.669 --> 00:30:40.338
We clean the windows, so
we need our suction cup.

00:30:40.338 --> 00:30:41.965
We bring what we call a diaper.

00:30:41.965 --> 00:30:44.217
Otherwise it just looks like a rag.

00:30:44.217 --> 00:30:46.553
And we clean the acrylic
windows with that.

00:30:46.553 --> 00:30:48.805
And then usually we're
doing hand-scrubbing.

00:30:48.805 --> 00:30:50.557
They are scrubbing off the algae.

00:30:50.557 --> 00:30:52.100
And they're kind of basically wafting off

00:30:52.100 --> 00:30:54.561
all that sand substrate that cakes on

00:30:54.561 --> 00:30:56.563
and falls over onto those rocks.

00:30:56.563 --> 00:30:58.273
All that stuff that can grow on there

00:30:58.273 --> 00:31:01.401
is potentially harmful for all the fish.

00:31:01.401 --> 00:31:03.361
So the whole goal of cleaning,
upkeeping the exhibit,

00:31:03.361 --> 00:31:05.363
isn't necessarily to keep it pretty,

00:31:05.363 --> 00:31:07.198
even though that is also very beneficial,

00:31:07.198 --> 00:31:09.200
to keep it pretty for the guests.

00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:11.953
But at the same time, there is the purpose

00:31:11.953 --> 00:31:14.956
of doing it for the health of the exhibit.

00:31:14.956 --> 00:31:16.499
This is a monster exhibit.

00:31:16.499 --> 00:31:17.750
So if if we didn't do it every day,

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:19.961
it'd be pretty much impossible to upkeep.

00:31:19.961 --> 00:31:22.213
The blitz dives, which
are our night dives,

00:31:22.213 --> 00:31:23.798
that's our essential deep clean.

00:31:25.216 --> 00:31:26.801
So it can be a couple hours long,

00:31:26.801 --> 00:31:28.219
just to get everything we can done.

00:31:28.219 --> 00:31:30.638
We can run up to four
of those Armada machines

00:31:30.638 --> 00:31:32.682
and clean different areas of the exhibit.

00:31:34.601 --> 00:31:37.437
Narrator: While Ocean
Voyager is being cleaned,

00:31:37.437 --> 00:31:40.064
Carolyn Murphy and the aquarist team

00:31:40.064 --> 00:31:42.400
are over at the River Scout gallery

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:46.988
getting ready to dive into
these three freshwater tanks.

00:31:46.988 --> 00:31:51.492
The smallest tanks measure
4 feet tall by 3 feet deep,

00:31:51.492 --> 00:31:55.246
and the team spends one
to two hours every week

00:31:55.246 --> 00:31:56.456
cleaning the windows

00:31:56.456 --> 00:31:59.042
and maintaining the
underwater gardens here.

00:31:59.042 --> 00:32:00.627
Carolyn: So despite the
size of these tanks,

00:32:00.627 --> 00:32:04.339
we actually do manage to
squeeze some people in there.

00:32:04.339 --> 00:32:07.008
What we are going to be doing is
something called hookah diving.

00:32:07.008 --> 00:32:09.093
So the divers are getting
in very carefully.

00:32:09.093 --> 00:32:11.304
They're kind of sliding
in the top of the tank.

00:32:11.304 --> 00:32:13.014
And they're going to be
holding themselves up

00:32:13.014 --> 00:32:15.642
in the water column by pressing their feet

00:32:15.642 --> 00:32:16.935
to opposite sides of the tank,

00:32:16.935 --> 00:32:19.520
or putting them on the
outside the top rim.

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:21.981
And they'll kind of hold
themselves with their core strength

00:32:21.981 --> 00:32:25.234
kind of up in the water column
to do the work they need to do.

00:32:25.234 --> 00:32:26.569
So we're focusing on the algae

00:32:26.569 --> 00:32:29.030
that's growing on the
window and the plant leaves,

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:31.407
and then also the detritus
that's building up in the tanks.

00:32:31.407 --> 00:32:34.911
And that can be fecals
from fish or leftover food.

00:32:34.911 --> 00:32:36.537
Narrator: The team goes into the dive

00:32:36.537 --> 00:32:40.458
with a few core tools: a
scrub brush, trimming tools,

00:32:40.458 --> 00:32:44.462
and a fine plastic scraper to
clean algae off the windows.

00:32:44.462 --> 00:32:48.383
The most painstaking part for
the aquarists in this tank

00:32:48.383 --> 00:32:50.593
is cleaning out the beard algae.

00:32:50.593 --> 00:32:52.637
Carolyn: So it is so
annoying, and it is something

00:32:52.637 --> 00:32:55.640
that we tediously try
to keep under control.

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:58.184
So you're seeing these
little kind of black puffs.

00:32:58.184 --> 00:32:59.686
And that's what she's targeting right now

00:32:59.686 --> 00:33:00.770
when she's scraping it off,

00:33:00.770 --> 00:33:02.772
and immediately removing
it from the system,

00:33:02.772 --> 00:33:05.858
because it will just
spread over everything.

00:33:07.402 --> 00:33:09.487
Narrator: The divers use a gravity siphon

00:33:09.487 --> 00:33:13.157
to clean out the loose debris
that's settled in the gravel.

00:33:13.157 --> 00:33:15.410
This isn't powered by electricity.

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:18.871
It relies solely on gravity to suction up

00:33:18.871 --> 00:33:21.374
and separate the waste from the gravel.

00:33:22.917 --> 00:33:24.919
Carolyn: Algae covering
everything doesn't look great.

00:33:24.919 --> 00:33:28.006
But it also can cause
problems for your fish.

00:33:28.006 --> 00:33:31.634
So algae blooms in the wild
can kind of take the oxygen

00:33:31.634 --> 00:33:33.511
that the fish need out of the system.

00:33:33.511 --> 00:33:38.016
So it can cause some
harm for them that way.

00:33:38.016 --> 00:33:39.183
Narrator: All the waste and debris

00:33:39.183 --> 00:33:41.894
from each of these cleanings ends up here.

00:33:41.894 --> 00:33:44.522
This is cleaned out every
three to four months

00:33:44.522 --> 00:33:46.816
to ensure the basin doesn't overflow,

00:33:46.816 --> 00:33:48.901
it doesn't create water-quality issues,

00:33:48.901 --> 00:33:51.946
and all the systems
continue to run smoothly.

00:33:51.946 --> 00:33:55.950
Rivers: As you can see,
we've got just piles of poop

00:33:55.950 --> 00:33:59.662
and debris and extra food,

00:33:59.662 --> 00:34:01.372
stuff that didn't get digested properly,

00:34:01.372 --> 00:34:05.043
stuff that didn't get eaten,
got pulled into filtration.

00:34:05.043 --> 00:34:07.879
Most of it is just animal waste.

00:34:07.879 --> 00:34:09.297
There is a little bit of sand,

00:34:09.297 --> 00:34:12.425
probably about 10 5-gallon buckets or so.

00:34:12.425 --> 00:34:14.385
The equipment we'll use to clean the basin

00:34:14.385 --> 00:34:18.056
is a fire hose and a snow shovel.

00:34:18.056 --> 00:34:19.015
Narrator: Cleaning this space

00:34:19.015 --> 00:34:22.727
takes roughly two to three
hours and is done in stages.

00:34:22.727 --> 00:34:24.937
They'll remove the poop, clean the floors,

00:34:24.937 --> 00:34:27.231
and then shovel all the sand out.

00:34:27.231 --> 00:34:28.941
Once we've gotten the sand out,

00:34:28.941 --> 00:34:31.402
then we'll go around
and inspect the walls,

00:34:31.402 --> 00:34:32.737
make sure there's no cracks,

00:34:32.737 --> 00:34:34.822
make sure the waterproofing is still good.

00:34:35.823 --> 00:34:37.492
So where all the discoloration happens

00:34:37.492 --> 00:34:39.327
is about where the basin gets,

00:34:39.327 --> 00:34:44.123
roughly 55,000 to 60,000
gallons to a full basin.

00:34:44.123 --> 00:34:45.625
But other than being gross,

00:34:45.625 --> 00:34:47.835
the hardest part is definitely
getting the sand out,

00:34:47.835 --> 00:34:50.797
just because we've got to
lift it 25 feet in the air.

00:34:50.797 --> 00:34:53.257
So we really only want to
fill them about half full.

00:34:54.258 --> 00:34:56.677
If they were to get too
full, they might splash.

00:34:56.677 --> 00:34:58.429
And they're also just really heavy.

00:34:59.722 --> 00:35:01.933
Once this basin is full, we will then

00:35:01.933 --> 00:35:05.603
recirculate all this water and
clean this water to reuse it

00:35:05.603 --> 00:35:08.022
for top-off water for our
Ocean Voyager exhibit.

00:35:08.022 --> 00:35:12.360
After the recovery, all
this water is crystal clean.

00:35:12.527 --> 00:35:14.570
Narrator: Next, we head to Niagara Falls,

00:35:14.570 --> 00:35:16.823
where special teams show us how they remove

00:35:16.823 --> 00:35:20.785
dangerous rocks and maintain 
the trails for visitors' safety.

00:35:24.455 --> 00:35:26.207
Narrator: This jagged sheet of rock

00:35:26.207 --> 00:35:29.335
is hanging 200 feet above a walking trail

00:35:29.335 --> 00:35:31.003
next to the Niagara River.

00:35:32.421 --> 00:35:34.674
It's already loose, and if it falls,

00:35:34.674 --> 00:35:38.386
it could mean serious injury
to hikers on the path below.

00:35:39.887 --> 00:35:42.640
That's where the rope
access team comes in.

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:44.809
If they do their job just right,

00:35:44.809 --> 00:35:48.312
most of Niagara Falls' 9
million yearly visitors

00:35:48.312 --> 00:35:50.648
will never even know they were here.

00:35:55.736 --> 00:35:57.822
Andy: First jump of the day.

00:35:59.157 --> 00:36:00.199
Andy: Am I scared?
Crew member: Yeah.

00:36:00.199 --> 00:36:01.951
Andy: Am I scared?!
Crew member: You should be.

00:36:01.951 --> 00:36:04.996
Andy: I have a healthy
respect. That's all.

00:36:07.248 --> 00:36:08.499
Narrator: This cliff is located

00:36:08.499 --> 00:36:10.209
at Devil's Hole State Park,

00:36:10.209 --> 00:36:13.838
roughly 4 miles north of Niagara Falls.

00:36:13.838 --> 00:36:16.966
Team members like Andy and
Christian will scale the cliff

00:36:16.966 --> 00:36:18.759
and maintain the crew's gear.

00:36:18.759 --> 00:36:22.054
Later, Jake will clear
dislodged rocks from the trail.

00:36:22.054 --> 00:36:23.472
If those rocks were to fall naturally,

00:36:23.472 --> 00:36:25.308
there's nothing stopping them from coming

00:36:25.308 --> 00:36:27.602
and just plowing through
somebody that's on the trail.

00:36:27.602 --> 00:36:29.812
So very necessary work, what we do here,

00:36:29.812 --> 00:36:31.606
and you can see here all the rocks

00:36:31.606 --> 00:36:33.274
that come down there are pretty sizable,

00:36:33.274 --> 00:36:35.568
and they could cause some damage.

00:36:35.568 --> 00:36:37.403
Narrator: It's a dangerous job.

00:36:37.403 --> 00:36:40.031
Dehydration, injury, and inclement weather

00:36:40.031 --> 00:36:42.450
are just some of the risks.

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:44.202
Thunderstorms are forecast today,

00:36:44.202 --> 00:36:47.288
so the team is on high alert
for signs of lightning,

00:36:47.288 --> 00:36:49.457
which would endanger the crew.

00:36:51.042 --> 00:36:53.336
Andy: We won't work in
lightning. That's about it.

00:36:54.879 --> 00:36:55.796
We're going to have fun.

00:36:55.796 --> 00:36:57.798
You know, usually when
we show this to people,

00:36:57.798 --> 00:37:00.301
they say they didn't
even know this existed.

00:37:00.301 --> 00:37:02.678
Narrator: There are 12
members of today's crew:

00:37:02.678 --> 00:37:06.307
four supervisors, including
Andy, and eight technicians.

00:37:07.600 --> 00:37:09.185
Today, their goal is to clear

00:37:09.185 --> 00:37:12.521
this entire section of the cliff.

00:37:12.521 --> 00:37:15.316
Their day begins at 7 a.m.
at the maintenance shop,

00:37:15.316 --> 00:37:17.818
where the team packs a
variety of gear into vehicles

00:37:17.818 --> 00:37:20.154
to take it to the access
point above the trail.

00:37:21.155 --> 00:37:22.448
Andy: See, the fun thing about this

00:37:22.448 --> 00:37:25.368
is that I don't actually
jump with them every day.

00:37:25.368 --> 00:37:28.120
So today will be one of
the days I jump with them,

00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:29.288
which is good because I've been

00:37:29.288 --> 00:37:30.915
wanting to do this for a while.

00:37:30.915 --> 00:37:33.584
These are the guys who
do it every single day,

00:37:33.584 --> 00:37:35.294
and they're really, really good at it.

00:37:36.254 --> 00:37:37.964
Narrator: After a short 10-minute drive,

00:37:37.964 --> 00:37:40.883
the team arrives at the access
point and begins gearing up.

00:37:43.219 --> 00:37:44.679
Christian: I guess we could start with

00:37:44.679 --> 00:37:46.973
the pièce de résistance.

00:37:46.973 --> 00:37:49.016
This is our climbing harness.

00:37:49.016 --> 00:37:50.768
It's a Class III harness,

00:37:50.768 --> 00:37:53.896
and it's called that because
of the shoulder straps.

00:37:53.896 --> 00:37:55.481
Narrator: The shoulder
straps prevent climbers

00:37:55.481 --> 00:37:57.108
from slipping out of the harness

00:37:57.108 --> 00:37:59.235
if they accidentally turn upside down,

00:37:59.235 --> 00:38:01.279
and the seat balances their body weight

00:38:01.279 --> 00:38:04.240
so that blood flow to their
legs isn't restricted.

00:38:04.240 --> 00:38:06.409
Comfort is also of the utmost importance,

00:38:06.409 --> 00:38:07.994
as climbers can be suspended

00:38:07.994 --> 00:38:10.246
for more than two hours at a time.

00:38:12.248 --> 00:38:14.083
Christian: This device
here is our bar rack.

00:38:14.083 --> 00:38:15.334
This is what we use to rappel.

00:38:15.334 --> 00:38:18.754
It's a pretty old-school device.

00:38:18.754 --> 00:38:20.548
We have a lot of mud and grit,

00:38:20.548 --> 00:38:22.591
and when the ropes get wet, they swell.

00:38:22.591 --> 00:38:25.678
So there's not a lot of moving
parts; it's all friction.

00:38:25.678 --> 00:38:27.471
So that's our descent device.

00:38:27.471 --> 00:38:30.933
Andy: All right, pretty
much all ready to go here.

00:38:30.933 --> 00:38:32.018
No one cares about me.

00:38:32.018 --> 00:38:34.979
Everyone says, "Andy, just
take care of yourself."

00:38:34.979 --> 00:38:36.731
[laughing]

00:38:36.731 --> 00:38:37.773
So, what they're talking about

00:38:37.773 --> 00:38:41.527
is some guys have a rope
handler watching their line,

00:38:41.527 --> 00:38:43.988
and then some of us are
on what we call an ASAP,

00:38:43.988 --> 00:38:46.032
which is this. This is your backup device.

00:38:46.032 --> 00:38:49.869
And so I'm just fully in
control of all of my descent.

00:38:49.869 --> 00:38:51.579
So I can actually step over the edge now.

00:38:57.293 --> 00:38:59.211
First jump of the day.

00:39:00.254 --> 00:39:01.464
Andy: Am I scared?
Crew member: Yeah.

00:39:01.464 --> 00:39:03.215
Andy: Am I scared?!
Crew member: You should be.

00:39:03.215 --> 00:39:05.718
Andy: I have a healthy
respect. That's all.

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:10.431
The crew's really close.

00:39:10.431 --> 00:39:12.224
You know, we joke around a lot,

00:39:12.224 --> 00:39:15.436
but when it is time to
actually go over the edge

00:39:15.436 --> 00:39:18.272
and do work, we kind
of call that game time,

00:39:18.272 --> 00:39:20.191
because it is. You know, you
need to take it seriously

00:39:20.191 --> 00:39:22.318
because there's a lot of risks involved.

00:39:22.318 --> 00:39:23.694
This program that we're a part of

00:39:23.694 --> 00:39:26.864
has been going on since about the 1930s,

00:39:26.864 --> 00:39:30.618
and about the whole time
they've been using pike poles.

00:39:30.618 --> 00:39:31.911
There's a lot of utility with this.

00:39:31.911 --> 00:39:34.205
This is made out of solid fiberglass here.

00:39:34.205 --> 00:39:36.457
So if it gets into the cliff,

00:39:36.457 --> 00:39:38.584
this can flex a lot.

00:39:38.584 --> 00:39:40.544
You can use this to position
yourself on the wall,

00:39:40.544 --> 00:39:43.798
and then you can use this
to gently remove debris.

00:39:43.798 --> 00:39:46.300
You can grab stuff with the
hook, gently pull it out.

00:39:46.300 --> 00:39:48.386
It can fit into cracks,

00:39:48.386 --> 00:39:50.554
and then you can get leverage that way.

00:39:51.639 --> 00:39:52.765
Narrator: One of the things that makes

00:39:52.765 --> 00:39:55.518
removing these rocks
especially challenging

00:39:55.518 --> 00:39:57.061
is that these cliffs undergo

00:39:57.061 --> 00:39:59.897
something called differential erosion,

00:39:59.897 --> 00:40:02.024
which means that areas of the cliff erode

00:40:02.024 --> 00:40:03.192
at different rates.

00:40:03.192 --> 00:40:06.445
Layers of shale rock located
lower on the cliffside

00:40:06.445 --> 00:40:07.696
wear away more quickly

00:40:07.696 --> 00:40:10.282
than the layer of
dolomite rock at the top.

00:40:10.282 --> 00:40:13.452
That leaves large unstable chunks of rock,

00:40:13.452 --> 00:40:18.374
sometimes the size of an SUV,
hanging at the cliff's edge.

00:40:18.374 --> 00:40:20.251
The crew uses a controlled swing

00:40:20.251 --> 00:40:23.295
to gain access to material
beneath overhangs.

00:40:23.295 --> 00:40:25.798
To do this, they push
themselves away from the wall

00:40:25.798 --> 00:40:28.759
with their feet to maintain
that controlled movement.

00:40:32.388 --> 00:40:34.390
These are softball shin pads,

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:38.018
and they work really,
really well for what we do.

00:40:38.018 --> 00:40:39.395
You try not to knee the wall,

00:40:39.395 --> 00:40:41.689
but sometimes for stability
purposes, you know,

00:40:41.689 --> 00:40:43.482
you can't really help it.

00:40:43.482 --> 00:40:46.318
We all try to wear steel toe
or some sort of composite toe.

00:40:46.318 --> 00:40:47.528
Rocks are hard.

00:40:47.528 --> 00:40:49.989
You know, you can't always
dodge a rock when you're moving,

00:40:49.989 --> 00:40:53.075
so having something like
that to keep yourself safe

00:40:53.075 --> 00:40:55.578
is 100% necessary.

00:40:58.247 --> 00:40:59.790
So, all the rocks that we're knocking down

00:40:59.790 --> 00:41:01.876
are essentially coming right
to the trail and then some,

00:41:01.876 --> 00:41:03.127
and they're going right
to the Niagara River,

00:41:03.127 --> 00:41:04.879
which we have right there below us.

00:41:05.880 --> 00:41:08.549
Narrator: It's 10 a.m.
The crew is only about

00:41:08.549 --> 00:41:10.509
two-thirds of the way through their climb

00:41:10.509 --> 00:41:14.138
when the one thing they're
worried about happens: a storm.

00:41:14.138 --> 00:41:15.097
[thunder striking]

00:41:15.097 --> 00:41:17.850
Crew member: Copy. Looks like
we might have a wall of rain.

00:41:17.850 --> 00:41:19.143
Cinematographer: Yeah,
I'm going to head back.

00:41:19.143 --> 00:41:20.853
Let's head back.

00:41:20.853 --> 00:41:22.980
Narrator: The crew hears
thunder and spots lightning

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:24.440
as they're on the cliffside.

00:41:24.440 --> 00:41:26.484
They need to stop working immediately.

00:41:27.735 --> 00:41:29.904
That will end their progress for today.

00:41:31.363 --> 00:41:32.948
Andy: So, today we
actually had to cut short.

00:41:32.948 --> 00:41:34.366
We have metal pike poles in our hand.

00:41:34.366 --> 00:41:35.409
We got a lot of metal gear on us,

00:41:35.409 --> 00:41:38.496
so we just tried to get out of
there as quickly as we could.

00:41:38.496 --> 00:41:41.081
Anything that's going to pose
a hazard to the employees,

00:41:41.081 --> 00:41:42.917
then we just stop.

00:41:42.917 --> 00:41:44.168
Narrator: The crew had only been working

00:41:44.168 --> 00:41:47.379
on the 200-foot cliffside
for less than an hour

00:41:47.379 --> 00:41:49.215
when the storm began.

00:41:49.215 --> 00:41:52.718
On a typical day, it could
take the crew up to three hours

00:41:52.718 --> 00:41:54.803
to clear a whole section.

00:41:54.803 --> 00:41:57.515
When they're able to resume,
they'll finish removing rocks

00:41:57.515 --> 00:41:59.058
from the lower section of the cliff

00:41:59.058 --> 00:42:02.478
and then stabilize the slope
side with wire meshing.

00:42:02.478 --> 00:42:05.648
This is the primary part of our work here,

00:42:05.648 --> 00:42:07.483
that we're going to try
and clear this all out

00:42:07.483 --> 00:42:10.861
so we can essentially have an
open hiking trail here again.

00:42:10.861 --> 00:42:12.071
And it's going to take a good bit of work

00:42:12.071 --> 00:42:13.948
and a good bit of time
to get this cleared out

00:42:13.948 --> 00:42:16.116
to be safe for us to open it again.

00:42:19.078 --> 00:42:19.912
Narrator: Back at the shop,

00:42:19.912 --> 00:42:22.957
the crew does paperwork
and washes their gear.

00:42:22.957 --> 00:42:24.500
Andy: There's actually a
lot of paperwork required

00:42:24.500 --> 00:42:25.626
with all this stuff,

00:42:25.626 --> 00:42:28.212
and then also just
keeping their gear clean.

00:42:31.006 --> 00:42:32.174
You can kind of see from my shirt,

00:42:32.174 --> 00:42:34.635
I'm filthy from one jump today.

00:42:34.635 --> 00:42:37.096
And, you know, if this is how I look,

00:42:37.096 --> 00:42:39.265
our gear is even worse.

00:42:39.265 --> 00:42:43.435
Christian: So, soapy water helps
break it and lift the dirt.

00:42:43.435 --> 00:42:45.896
Using a toothbrush too to get in there,

00:42:45.896 --> 00:42:48.524
and then using an air compressor to spray

00:42:48.524 --> 00:42:51.860
any of the soap and dirt that's in there.

00:42:51.860 --> 00:42:54.238
And then we have a
lubricant too when it's dry

00:42:54.238 --> 00:42:58.492
to get in there, and it
prevents rust and corrosion.

00:42:59.618 --> 00:43:02.329
Narrator: After the rope access
team has made some progress,

00:43:02.329 --> 00:43:03.872
it's time to start clearing the rocks

00:43:03.872 --> 00:43:05.958
from the walking paths below.

00:43:05.958 --> 00:43:08.419
This is where the gorge crew comes in.

00:43:10.963 --> 00:43:14.091
Jake: We take care of
about seven trails in total

00:43:14.091 --> 00:43:16.176
within the gorge itself

00:43:16.176 --> 00:43:18.220
and then two up top, on topside.

00:43:18.220 --> 00:43:20.973
Now we are in the process
of cleaning the trail

00:43:20.973 --> 00:43:22.099
and rebuilding it.

00:43:22.099 --> 00:43:23.684
We've already come down,

00:43:23.684 --> 00:43:26.061
cleared the loose debris off the trail,

00:43:26.061 --> 00:43:28.063
and then started staging to get ready

00:43:28.063 --> 00:43:31.150
for the bigger jobs ahead.

00:43:31.150 --> 00:43:32.109
Narrator: One of those jobs

00:43:32.109 --> 00:43:34.945
includes a specialized
stabilization technique

00:43:34.945 --> 00:43:37.114
to ensure the slope side is secure.

00:43:38.282 --> 00:43:39.992
Jake: So, down here, to fix these trails,

00:43:39.992 --> 00:43:42.161
we use gabion baskets.

00:43:42.161 --> 00:43:45.581
It's a 6-foot-by-3-foot cube

00:43:45.581 --> 00:43:48.083
that is made of wire meshing.

00:43:48.083 --> 00:43:50.085
And what you do is you dig them down,

00:43:50.085 --> 00:43:53.088
place them within the trail
or wherever you need them,

00:43:53.088 --> 00:43:55.466
and then you add rock to the inside

00:43:55.466 --> 00:43:57.426
so that the rock weighs it down.

00:43:57.426 --> 00:44:00.679
A job like this can take a week or two,

00:44:00.679 --> 00:44:04.725
but if we have a good day
where we're really rocking

00:44:04.725 --> 00:44:06.268
and getting stuff done,

00:44:06.268 --> 00:44:09.438
it can be a maybe three- to four-day job.

00:44:09.438 --> 00:44:11.440
Christian: This mound right
here, if all hands are on deck,

00:44:11.440 --> 00:44:13.817
I'd like to see, hopefully
by the end of the summer

00:44:13.817 --> 00:44:15.152
we get a majority of it taken out

00:44:15.152 --> 00:44:18.072
and then we have put in some
stabilization techniques

00:44:18.072 --> 00:44:20.074
to help build the trail back up.

00:44:20.074 --> 00:44:21.867
So I could definitely see
us working into the fall,

00:44:21.867 --> 00:44:22.868
and if I had to make a guess,

00:44:22.868 --> 00:44:24.620
it'll probably be open next summer.

00:44:25.954 --> 00:44:27.581
Andy: I think if you're going
to do this kind of work,

00:44:27.581 --> 00:44:30.876
at the core, you have to
enjoy working outdoors.

00:44:30.876 --> 00:44:32.628
You get to see special
things that people don't see,

00:44:32.628 --> 00:44:35.506
plants and animals, the landscape.

00:44:35.506 --> 00:44:37.633
The work that you do, it's really hard,

00:44:37.633 --> 00:44:40.844
but then you're helping
keep the park safe.

00:44:40.844 --> 00:44:42.763
You make a meaningful difference.

00:44:42.763 --> 00:44:43.889
If you're just a park user,

00:44:43.889 --> 00:44:46.016
you might not even realize
we were ever there,

00:44:46.016 --> 00:44:47.893
and that's kind of the point.

00:44:51.397 --> 00:44:54.024
Narrator: This is Mack.
He specializes in cleaning

00:44:54.024 --> 00:44:56.318
homes of people with hoarding disorder

00:44:56.318 --> 00:45:00.698
and is used to wading through
heaping piles of clutter,

00:45:00.698 --> 00:45:04.785
but this job presents an
unusually large challenge.

00:45:04.785 --> 00:45:07.955
Mack will be cleaning a
six-bedroom home in Illinois,

00:45:07.955 --> 00:45:10.833
and it's the biggest he's ever tackled.

00:45:10.833 --> 00:45:13.043
This is just a nightmare.

00:45:14.253 --> 00:45:17.131
I mean, we're finding food from 2013,

00:45:17.131 --> 00:45:18.465
we've got paint cans that I

00:45:18.465 --> 00:45:20.384
no longer have the strength to carry.

00:45:22.970 --> 00:45:24.346
Narrator: Mack often cleans solo

00:45:24.346 --> 00:45:26.682
and does much of this work free of charge,

00:45:26.682 --> 00:45:29.685
but this job is so
daunting that he's invited

00:45:29.685 --> 00:45:31.895
two other cleaners to join him.

00:45:31.895 --> 00:45:34.481
Over the next three days,
they'll work together

00:45:34.481 --> 00:45:37.568
to declutter and clean as
many rooms as possible.

00:45:38.777 --> 00:45:40.696
Mack: Normally I'm dealing

00:45:40.696 --> 00:45:42.823
with a totally different type of cleaning.

00:45:42.823 --> 00:45:44.825
In other hoarder houses, I'm dealing

00:45:44.825 --> 00:45:49.288
with a lot of insects and
mouse droppings and pet urine

00:45:49.288 --> 00:45:51.665
and just a lot of stuff
that you definitely want

00:45:51.665 --> 00:45:55.085
to have PPE on in order
to even enter the house.

00:45:55.085 --> 00:45:59.339
This one was just piles
upon piles of stuff.

00:45:59.339 --> 00:46:02.718
It's the most stuff that I've
ever taken out of a house.

00:46:05.637 --> 00:46:07.723
Narrator: This house
belongs to a family of four,

00:46:07.723 --> 00:46:11.518
who declined to appear on
camera due to privacy reasons.

00:46:11.518 --> 00:46:13.353
One of the family members is believed

00:46:13.353 --> 00:46:14.980
to be living with hoarding disorder,

00:46:14.980 --> 00:46:17.941
although he's awaiting
an official diagnosis.

00:46:17.941 --> 00:46:21.862
But even with treatment,
experts say relapses can occur.

00:46:21.862 --> 00:46:25.073
This is the fourth time
cleaners have worked extensively

00:46:25.073 --> 00:46:26.533
to declutter this home.

00:46:26.533 --> 00:46:29.745
The last attempt was five years ago.

00:46:29.745 --> 00:46:32.790
Mack: Rooms in hoarder houses,
we will often find rooms

00:46:32.790 --> 00:46:35.459
that have never been entered in a decade.

00:46:35.459 --> 00:46:39.338
And one of the reasons that
happens, and it's really common,

00:46:39.338 --> 00:46:42.883
is you have a room that
no longer has a purpose.

00:46:42.883 --> 00:46:45.219
So in this room, it's the living room,

00:46:45.219 --> 00:46:47.888
and the purpose is to
watch TV and to relax.

00:46:47.888 --> 00:46:49.723
If a room loses that purpose

00:46:49.723 --> 00:46:52.392
because they start piling things up,

00:46:52.392 --> 00:46:54.311
then the purpose of the room becomes

00:46:54.311 --> 00:46:57.105
this is where we throw all of our stuff.

00:46:57.105 --> 00:47:00.567
This place is not what I'd
consider a hazard or a biohazard.

00:47:00.567 --> 00:47:02.986
It's a hazard for tripping.

00:47:04.446 --> 00:47:06.114
And it's a hazard for fire,

00:47:06.114 --> 00:47:08.033
because they've got an
open fireplace in here,

00:47:08.033 --> 00:47:11.078
and they've got so much clutter
that it becomes a danger.

00:47:11.078 --> 00:47:13.413
Narrator: Hoarding is defined
as "persistent difficulty

00:47:13.413 --> 00:47:15.541
discarding or parting with possessions,

00:47:15.541 --> 00:47:17.292
regardless of their actual value."

00:47:17.292 --> 00:47:20.504
It's estimated that
somewhere between 2 and 4%

00:47:20.504 --> 00:47:23.131
of the worldwide population
have hoarding disorder.

00:47:24.132 --> 00:47:27.010
It's only day one of
this ambitious project,

00:47:27.010 --> 00:47:29.471
but when Mack and his
team arrive at the house,

00:47:29.471 --> 00:47:33.100
they discover things already
aren't going as planned.

00:47:33.100 --> 00:47:35.435
Mack: So, they have
cleaned a bunch of stuff

00:47:35.435 --> 00:47:38.981
before we got here, which
normally I wouldn't like.

00:47:38.981 --> 00:47:41.859
I wanted the big mess,
and I wanted to be able

00:47:41.859 --> 00:47:44.736
to go through the whole house
and make it pretty myself.

00:47:44.736 --> 00:47:47.447
This is, I think, a 30-yard dumpster.

00:47:47.447 --> 00:47:50.993
They've filled two of
these already over the top,

00:47:50.993 --> 00:47:53.161
and we're getting ready
to fill the third one.

00:47:53.161 --> 00:47:55.664
If we had more time,
we would probably fill

00:47:55.664 --> 00:47:57.583
10 of these from this house.

00:47:57.583 --> 00:47:59.793
So even though I'm a little disappointed,

00:47:59.793 --> 00:48:03.005
I'm really thankful that
they did have the help.

00:48:03.005 --> 00:48:04.590
Narrator: Mack and his
team plan to start on

00:48:04.590 --> 00:48:08.051
what's typically the hardest
room to clean, the kitchen.

00:48:08.051 --> 00:48:10.554
They'll need to pay special
attention to this space

00:48:10.554 --> 00:48:13.807
to ensure it's sanitary
enough for handling food.

00:48:14.766 --> 00:48:16.143
Then they'll move on to the rooms

00:48:16.143 --> 00:48:17.769
on the main level of the house

00:48:17.769 --> 00:48:19.897
and finally tackle the basement,

00:48:19.897 --> 00:48:23.108
which hasn't been entered in three years.

00:48:23.108 --> 00:48:25.027
The key tools Mack and his team use

00:48:25.027 --> 00:48:27.696
are an emulsifier to cut through grease,

00:48:27.696 --> 00:48:30.949
a homemade all-purpose
cleaner, scouring pads,

00:48:30.949 --> 00:48:33.827
and razor-blade scrapers.

00:48:33.827 --> 00:48:35.037
Barbie: So, when people are asking

00:48:35.037 --> 00:48:36.538
why this job takes so long,

00:48:36.538 --> 00:48:38.540
because we have to check everything,

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:40.500
like if it's expired or not

00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:41.919
or still good, no.

00:48:43.211 --> 00:48:44.922
Mack: Let me get you a cleaner.

00:48:44.922 --> 00:48:49.635
OK. Nope, a big no for the old pancakes.

00:48:51.136 --> 00:48:53.013
Nope.

00:48:53.013 --> 00:48:56.141
Beer. We never waste any beer, no.

00:49:00.812 --> 00:49:03.482
Mack: Holy crap. That was a lot.

00:49:04.650 --> 00:49:06.193
[grunts]

00:49:08.946 --> 00:49:11.740
Yeah, that's pushing an easy 200 pounds.

00:49:15.994 --> 00:49:18.038
Narrator: Mack says the biggest challenge

00:49:18.038 --> 00:49:21.166
of cleaning a house like
this is knowing what to keep

00:49:21.166 --> 00:49:22.459
and what to throw away.

00:49:23.669 --> 00:49:25.045
Mack: There are a lot
of people who will say,

00:49:25.045 --> 00:49:27.339
"You're fine to throw away
absolutely everything,"

00:49:27.339 --> 00:49:29.049
but they don't really mean that.

00:49:29.049 --> 00:49:31.301
They mean you can throw away the junk,

00:49:31.301 --> 00:49:34.471
but you have to decipher
what's junk and what's not.

00:49:34.471 --> 00:49:37.099
There's also the urge to throw away

00:49:37.099 --> 00:49:40.560
large armfuls of stuff,
and most of the time

00:49:40.560 --> 00:49:41.770
there's going to be some stuff

00:49:41.770 --> 00:49:44.314
underneath it that's important.

00:49:44.314 --> 00:49:46.274
All right, I got one
counter done. I'm done.

00:49:46.274 --> 00:49:47.526
Barbie: Beautiful!

00:49:47.526 --> 00:49:49.027
Mack: Back to the hotel.

00:49:49.027 --> 00:49:49.861
[Barbie laughs]

00:49:50.696 --> 00:49:52.781
Narrator: Although cleaning
the home will make it safer

00:49:52.781 --> 00:49:55.450
and more livable, it can
be distressing for people

00:49:55.450 --> 00:49:58.870
with hoarding disorder to
see their space transformed.

00:49:58.870 --> 00:50:00.998
People who hoard tend to
have a deep connection

00:50:00.998 --> 00:50:02.916
with their objects, and removing them

00:50:02.916 --> 00:50:05.085
can create an experience of loss,

00:50:05.085 --> 00:50:06.878
which is one of the major contributors

00:50:06.878 --> 00:50:08.922
of hoarding behaviors.

00:50:08.922 --> 00:50:10.590
Mack: He's going to come home to a very,

00:50:10.590 --> 00:50:14.052
very dramatic change, and it's
going to be a shock to him.

00:50:14.052 --> 00:50:15.929
And even though most people will come home

00:50:15.929 --> 00:50:17.681
and see the change and think, "Oh, my God,

00:50:17.681 --> 00:50:19.808
this is so great, you've
cleaned my house,"

00:50:19.808 --> 00:50:22.060
he's probably not going to feel that way.

00:50:26.773 --> 00:50:28.650
Narrator: As day one comes to a close,

00:50:28.650 --> 00:50:30.986
Mack and the team have
finished most of the kitchen,

00:50:30.986 --> 00:50:34.698
which they will continue to
clean for the next two days.

00:50:34.698 --> 00:50:37.492
On day two, Barbie and
Bonnie will also tackle

00:50:37.492 --> 00:50:39.119
the sunroom and living room

00:50:39.119 --> 00:50:41.371
while Mack ventures into the basement.

00:50:42.998 --> 00:50:44.624
Mack: I want to go down to that basement

00:50:44.624 --> 00:50:47.836
and do as much as I can
on the basement today.

00:50:47.836 --> 00:50:51.006
That is going to completely wear me out.

00:50:51.006 --> 00:50:52.841
If I can get that out of the way today,

00:50:52.841 --> 00:50:54.509
then that means tomorrow we can do things

00:50:54.509 --> 00:50:58.680
like the dining room
and get this floor done.

00:51:06.396 --> 00:51:11.276
I'm going to attempt to tackle
this area, this whole area.

00:51:11.276 --> 00:51:12.986
There's this part of the basement,

00:51:12.986 --> 00:51:17.074
there is a room to my left,
and there's a room over here,

00:51:17.074 --> 00:51:19.034
and they all look like what's behind me.

00:51:19.034 --> 00:51:20.744
This is going to be extremely difficult

00:51:20.744 --> 00:51:24.081
because there is a lot of
things that need to be kept.

00:51:24.081 --> 00:51:25.749
Narrator: Mack discovers an abundance

00:51:25.749 --> 00:51:27.834
of stockpiled food and supplies,

00:51:27.834 --> 00:51:29.961
including countless jugs of water,

00:51:29.961 --> 00:51:33.507
jars of marinara sauce,
bottles of hydrogen peroxide,

00:51:33.507 --> 00:51:37.302
and 5-gallon containers
filled with something unusual.

00:51:37.302 --> 00:51:39.012
All these five gallon buckets,

00:51:39.012 --> 00:51:41.139
those are all filled with food.

00:51:41.139 --> 00:51:42.724
Yeah, I thought they were paint.

00:51:42.724 --> 00:51:43.809
Barbie: What kind?

00:51:43.809 --> 00:51:46.394
Mack: That one says macaroni
and spaghetti or something.

00:51:46.394 --> 00:51:48.355
So I'm just going to
stack them all together,

00:51:48.355 --> 00:51:49.940
and they want to keep them.

00:51:49.940 --> 00:51:51.108
Barbie: But do you think it's still good?

00:51:51.108 --> 00:51:52.526
Mack: Probably not.

00:51:52.526 --> 00:51:54.361
Narrator: People can
inherit characteristics

00:51:54.361 --> 00:51:56.530
from their parents that
make them more likely

00:51:56.530 --> 00:51:58.281
to develop hoarding behaviors.

00:51:58.281 --> 00:52:01.368
Grief and loss can also
contribute to this.

00:52:01.368 --> 00:52:03.787
Over the years, Mack has noticed patterns

00:52:03.787 --> 00:52:06.331
in the kinds of items
he finds while cleaning.

00:52:06.331 --> 00:52:09.417
Mack: They stack up air
filters and medication

00:52:09.417 --> 00:52:13.547
and antibiotics and hydrogen
peroxide and alcohol,

00:52:13.547 --> 00:52:16.466
and all of them are unopened,
and all of them are there

00:52:16.466 --> 00:52:19.928
just in case, and they just
keep building and building.

00:52:19.928 --> 00:52:21.346
Narrator: As Mack continues sorting

00:52:21.346 --> 00:52:23.807
through jugs of water
and medical supplies,

00:52:23.807 --> 00:52:26.059
he stumbles on one item in particular

00:52:26.059 --> 00:52:27.853
he's learned to never throw away.

00:52:27.853 --> 00:52:31.064
I've thrown away shells
twice, and in both occasions,

00:52:31.064 --> 00:52:33.358
people were like, "Have you
seen a big bag of shells?

00:52:33.358 --> 00:52:35.485
That was from when we went
to Myrtle Beach or Florida

00:52:35.485 --> 00:52:36.987
or whatever on a family trip."

00:52:36.987 --> 00:52:39.197
But they're never
displayed as a collection,

00:52:39.197 --> 00:52:42.367
they're just stuffed in a
bag in some random spot.

00:52:42.367 --> 00:52:44.244
But I've learned over time that shells

00:52:44.244 --> 00:52:46.746
are always a memento for somebody.

00:52:46.746 --> 00:52:49.958
Unless they've got their
house infested by clams,

00:52:49.958 --> 00:52:51.751
in which case they're good to go.

00:52:51.751 --> 00:52:53.587
Narrator: Much of what
Mack finds in the basement

00:52:53.587 --> 00:52:55.964
needs to be lugged
upstairs to the dumpsters,

00:52:55.964 --> 00:52:59.176
and so many trips up and down
the stairs with heavy bags

00:52:59.176 --> 00:53:01.178
is starting to take its toll.

00:53:01.178 --> 00:53:04.514
Mack: Right now my neck is just throbbing,

00:53:04.514 --> 00:53:06.725
and my right arm I'm
pretty sure is injured.

00:53:06.725 --> 00:53:10.061
I have to be super careful
today on what I lift

00:53:10.061 --> 00:53:12.939
and how I lift it and how much I lift.

00:53:12.939 --> 00:53:17.194
It's a little scary because
if I tweak my back any more,

00:53:17.194 --> 00:53:21.323
I'm out of the picture, and
I don't want that to happen.

00:53:21.323 --> 00:53:23.450
Narrator: Back upstairs, Barbie and Bonnie

00:53:23.450 --> 00:53:24.618
are finishing the kitchen

00:53:24.618 --> 00:53:26.995
and starting on the living
room and the sunroom.

00:53:28.914 --> 00:53:31.082
Bonnie: I wish I had time
to clean all of this,

00:53:31.082 --> 00:53:33.710
but, priorities.

00:53:35.921 --> 00:53:38.506
Mack: The owner used to
sit out in the sunroom

00:53:38.506 --> 00:53:43.511
every morning and have scrambled
eggs and toast and coffee.

00:53:43.595 --> 00:53:45.096
She likes to bead.

00:53:45.096 --> 00:53:47.390
And I like knowing those
details about the owner

00:53:47.390 --> 00:53:51.228
because it lets us know where emotionally

00:53:51.228 --> 00:53:54.522
this cleanup is going to
affect them in a positive way.

00:53:54.522 --> 00:53:56.775
Narrator: As the crew begins
to wrap up for the day,

00:53:56.775 --> 00:53:58.818
the wife of the person
suspected to be living

00:53:58.818 --> 00:54:01.029
with hoarding disorder becomes emotional

00:54:01.029 --> 00:54:03.531
at the sight of the progress they've made.

00:54:03.531 --> 00:54:05.659
Mack: She was on the
second floor of the house

00:54:05.659 --> 00:54:07.118
looking over the banister,

00:54:07.118 --> 00:54:09.913
and just seeing what
Barbie had accomplished,

00:54:09.913 --> 00:54:13.500
and she wasn't even
fully done with the room,

00:54:13.500 --> 00:54:15.043
she broke down in tears

00:54:15.043 --> 00:54:17.170
just being able to see the floor again.

00:54:18.588 --> 00:54:21.466
So if your partner has hoarding disorder,

00:54:21.466 --> 00:54:24.261
there is a resentment and
a frustration and an anger

00:54:24.261 --> 00:54:27.597
that builds up, and then
finally there's a release point

00:54:27.597 --> 00:54:29.766
where everything just comes out.

00:54:29.766 --> 00:54:31.434
And that's what happened there.

00:54:31.434 --> 00:54:34.646
She is very happy to see it opened up.

00:54:34.646 --> 00:54:37.274
She's been in the house for 13 years.

00:54:37.274 --> 00:54:40.068
Today's work was way more
exhausting than yesterday.

00:54:40.068 --> 00:54:41.653
It's a different type of cleaning, though,

00:54:41.653 --> 00:54:45.490
because it takes less of
your mind to do it, I guess.

00:54:45.490 --> 00:54:48.368
I'm not really looking
at having to sanitize

00:54:48.368 --> 00:54:50.704
and to get out tiny little stains.

00:54:50.704 --> 00:54:53.707
I'm just looking to get
things out of people's way.

00:54:53.707 --> 00:54:56.376
And so it takes less mental effort,

00:54:56.376 --> 00:55:00.005
but, man, it takes a lot
more physical effort.

00:55:00.005 --> 00:55:01.715
Narrator: It's the final day of the crew's

00:55:01.715 --> 00:55:05.051
three-day cleanup, and
this is their last chance

00:55:05.051 --> 00:55:06.636
to organize and declutter

00:55:06.636 --> 00:55:08.972
as much of the main floor as possible.

00:55:08.972 --> 00:55:11.016
Mack: This is the day where we have to be

00:55:11.016 --> 00:55:13.935
basically on our game and moving quickly.

00:55:13.935 --> 00:55:15.729
We're wrapping up the kitchen.

00:55:15.729 --> 00:55:18.189
We have a dining room which is just stuff

00:55:18.189 --> 00:55:20.275
that needs to be moved.

00:55:20.275 --> 00:55:23.111
Narrator: Despite the
exhaustion Mack is experiencing,

00:55:23.111 --> 00:55:26.698
he returns to the basement
and makes another discovery.

00:55:26.698 --> 00:55:28.450
Whew, God.

00:55:28.450 --> 00:55:30.618
It's, like, old rotten garden seeds,

00:55:32.329 --> 00:55:35.749
and they're not dangerous,

00:55:35.749 --> 00:55:37.542
but they smell really bad.

00:55:37.542 --> 00:55:40.503
Garden seeds, when they go
bad, smell like raw sewage.

00:55:40.503 --> 00:55:41.755
You have to breathe through your mouth

00:55:41.755 --> 00:55:43.673
when you're putting it in,

00:55:43.673 --> 00:55:46.092
otherwise it'll make you nauseous.

00:55:47.344 --> 00:55:49.387
You'll be able to see the story unfold

00:55:49.387 --> 00:55:52.682
as you go through layer
after layer of old stuff.

00:55:52.682 --> 00:55:54.059
The deeper you go into a hoard,

00:55:54.059 --> 00:55:56.728
that's where you start finding
the really rotten stuff.

00:55:56.728 --> 00:56:00.106
And so it always gets worse
before it gets better.

00:56:00.106 --> 00:56:01.900
Narrator: Excessive acquisition occurs

00:56:01.900 --> 00:56:04.152
in the vast majority of cases.

00:56:04.152 --> 00:56:06.654
Oftentimes these items are on sale,

00:56:06.654 --> 00:56:08.907
and therefore are bought in large amounts.

00:56:10.241 --> 00:56:12.243
Mack: So instead of just getting one,

00:56:12.243 --> 00:56:14.746
they see the deal and they'll get 20.

00:56:14.746 --> 00:56:16.873
But even in a big house like this,

00:56:16.873 --> 00:56:18.792
there's not room to put them all.

00:56:18.792 --> 00:56:21.127
So you've got this giant
pile of shop lights,

00:56:21.127 --> 00:56:23.380
and you're probably going
to find that with everything

00:56:23.380 --> 00:56:27.759
from craft supplies to
just random kind of stuff.

00:56:28.885 --> 00:56:29.844
Nightlights.

00:56:30.762 --> 00:56:33.139
There's an entire rack of nightlights.

00:56:33.139 --> 00:56:35.016
What's really frustrating about this

00:56:35.016 --> 00:56:38.186
is there's so much stuff that's necessary

00:56:38.186 --> 00:56:41.106
and has legitimate, actual use.

00:56:41.106 --> 00:56:45.193
That's actually really
rare for a hoarder house.

00:56:45.193 --> 00:56:46.486
Narrator: At the end of day three,

00:56:46.486 --> 00:56:48.738
the crew has successfully
cleaned the kitchen,

00:56:48.738 --> 00:56:52.367
sunroom, living room,
dining room, and basement,

00:56:52.367 --> 00:56:54.369
and they've removed enough stuff

00:56:54.369 --> 00:56:58.206
to fill three 30-yard
dumpsters to the brim.

00:56:58.206 --> 00:57:00.375
Mack: We're almost done now.

00:57:00.375 --> 00:57:03.962
Compared to other hoarder
houses that's just me,

00:57:03.962 --> 00:57:05.713
with three of us working here,

00:57:05.713 --> 00:57:09.134
in order to get the entire
house, every single room,

00:57:09.134 --> 00:57:11.886
top to bottom, cleaned, we
would have needed a crew

00:57:11.886 --> 00:57:16.266
of probably 10-plus people
for at least two weeks.

00:57:16.266 --> 00:57:17.976
Narrator: After Mack and his team leave,

00:57:17.976 --> 00:57:20.645
the family will finish
cleaning the remaining rooms.

00:57:20.645 --> 00:57:24.065
They'll also hire help
to maintain the home.

00:57:24.065 --> 00:57:27.819
Mack: I like seeing a house
that is so covered up with stuff

00:57:27.819 --> 00:57:30.864
that whenever you get all of
it put away and where it goes

00:57:30.864 --> 00:57:32.574
and you get everything cleaned up,

00:57:32.574 --> 00:57:36.286
seeing the house as it
was intended to be seen

00:57:36.286 --> 00:57:37.996
is really a cool thing to me,

00:57:37.996 --> 00:57:40.415
and I think that's part of my brain

00:57:40.415 --> 00:57:43.168
that thinks of rooms like jigsaw puzzles.

00:57:43.168 --> 00:57:44.919
What keeps me doing it, I think,

00:57:44.919 --> 00:57:48.465
is more the stress relief
that you see happen

00:57:48.465 --> 00:57:49.841
on the owner of the house.

00:57:49.841 --> 00:57:52.635
You can see the stress come off of them

00:57:52.635 --> 00:57:54.512
at the very end whenever you leave.

00:57:54.512 --> 00:57:57.557
This one was just
exhausting in every measure

00:57:57.557 --> 00:57:59.934
just because of the sheer amount of stuff.

00:57:59.934 --> 00:58:02.020
But it's worth it,
especially whenever we get

00:58:02.020 --> 00:58:03.354
to look around the house and say,

00:58:03.354 --> 00:58:06.816
"Holy crap, we did that. We all did that."

00:58:27.837 --> 00:58:29.756
Narrator : Now, we travel to the UK,

00:58:29.756 --> 00:58:32.467
where Oli Pickles, the owner of iDoctor,

00:58:32.467 --> 00:58:35.845
shows us how he cleans the dirtiest iPhones.

00:58:38.139 --> 00:58:41.142
I think the most difficult
part of cleaning an iPhone

00:58:41.142 --> 00:58:42.977
is just knowing that you're touching

00:58:42.977 --> 00:58:45.438
somebody else's grime and muck.

00:58:45.438 --> 00:58:47.899
It's disgusting, really,
when you think about it.

00:58:49.484 --> 00:58:51.945
I can already see that we've
got quite a lot of gunk

00:58:51.945 --> 00:58:55.448
in the ear speaker and the
speaker grilles themselves,

00:58:55.448 --> 00:58:58.701
so hopefully there'll be
some gunk in there to see

00:58:58.701 --> 00:59:00.954
and we can give it a real
good clean-out as well.

00:59:05.291 --> 00:59:07.669
Lint builds up from
inside somebody's pocket,

00:59:07.669 --> 00:59:11.256
and over time, when the
charging cable's inserted,

00:59:11.256 --> 00:59:13.466
taken out, reinserted, all that happens

00:59:13.466 --> 00:59:15.969
is it just compacts into
the charge port itself

00:59:15.969 --> 00:59:18.137
and leaves this sort of mess behind

00:59:18.137 --> 00:59:19.389
and all this lint.

00:59:19.389 --> 00:59:21.224
Over time, it just compacts.

00:59:21.224 --> 00:59:22.350
Every time you put it on charge,

00:59:22.350 --> 00:59:24.519
it just compacts deep down into the port.

00:59:28.022 --> 00:59:31.234
When you start noticing the
ear speaker becomes quiet,

00:59:31.234 --> 00:59:33.027
the microphone, people can't hear you,

00:59:33.027 --> 00:59:34.988
or it's not charging correctly,

00:59:34.988 --> 00:59:36.322
you might have to stand on your head

00:59:36.322 --> 00:59:39.158
and do a backflip just to make it charge,

00:59:39.158 --> 00:59:41.035
that's probably when you
need to start thinking

00:59:41.035 --> 00:59:43.705
about cleaning out that charge
port or the ear speaker.

00:59:44.581 --> 00:59:46.583
I'm using this little BGA scraper,

00:59:46.583 --> 00:59:50.753
which is typically designed
for scraping the resin overfill

00:59:50.753 --> 00:59:52.589
or underfill from logic boards,

00:59:52.589 --> 00:59:55.633
but it doubles up as a
really, really useful tool

00:59:55.633 --> 00:59:57.510
for cleaning out these charging ports.

01:00:03.099 --> 01:00:05.893
The stuff, what we usually
find in the charging ports

01:00:05.893 --> 01:00:09.564
and crevices of the phone,
is mostly either pocket gunk

01:00:09.564 --> 01:00:11.649
or fluff from the bottom of handbags.

01:00:11.649 --> 01:00:13.484
I mean, I'm not a forensic scientist,

01:00:13.484 --> 01:00:17.488
but I'm going to guess there's dog hairs,

01:00:17.488 --> 01:00:18.906
dust, fluff.

01:00:18.906 --> 01:00:23.077
I don't really know
how to describe the ...

01:00:23.077 --> 01:00:25.496
the fibers what are in there.

01:00:25.496 --> 01:00:27.123
Other things that we've
found in the phones,

01:00:27.123 --> 01:00:31.002
rice is quite common when phones
have become water damaged,

01:00:31.002 --> 01:00:33.129
because they'll stick the phone in rice.

01:00:33.129 --> 01:00:34.839
That doesn't work, by the way.

01:00:34.839 --> 01:00:36.799
We did find some weed in one of them,

01:00:36.799 --> 01:00:40.720
and it looked like they must
have been keeping the stash

01:00:40.720 --> 01:00:43.806
in the pocket, and it,
the same thing again.

01:00:43.806 --> 01:00:45.683
There was weed inside the charge port.

01:00:53.650 --> 01:00:56.944
So, this iPhone 7 has come
in for a battery replacement,

01:00:56.944 --> 01:00:58.154
and as you can see,

01:00:58.154 --> 01:01:01.407
it's absolutely full of dust,

01:01:01.407 --> 01:01:03.951
lint, hair, wood chip, feathers;

01:01:03.951 --> 01:01:06.079
you name it, it's in this phone.

01:01:06.079 --> 01:01:08.873
Well, I can see that the
screen's been replaced before.

01:01:08.873 --> 01:01:11.334
I think they've probably
just never replaced

01:01:11.334 --> 01:01:13.878
the dust seal what's on there.

01:01:13.878 --> 01:01:17.382
This is really common
because, believe it or not,

01:01:17.382 --> 01:01:20.885
not many repair places will replace

01:01:20.885 --> 01:01:22.595
that dust and moisture seal.

01:01:22.595 --> 01:01:24.806
The best way to clean
something like this out

01:01:24.806 --> 01:01:27.558
is to get all the thick stuff,

01:01:27.558 --> 01:01:29.686
probably with the dust blower.

01:01:34.899 --> 01:01:36.317
There is definitely a risk

01:01:36.317 --> 01:01:40.154
that this dust could cause a short circuit

01:01:40.154 --> 01:01:41.739
on the motherboard.

01:01:41.739 --> 01:01:43.491
It's going to put lots of strain

01:01:43.491 --> 01:01:46.786
on peripheral hardware
like the loudspeaker.

01:01:46.786 --> 01:01:47.829
It's going to be quiet.

01:01:47.829 --> 01:01:50.123
The vibration motor's not
going to work very well,

01:01:50.123 --> 01:01:51.749
or you're going to get funny sounds

01:01:51.749 --> 01:01:53.042
from the vibration motor.

01:01:53.042 --> 01:01:55.878
A lot of people think that
it's just for moisture.

01:01:55.878 --> 01:01:57.672
It keeps a seal between the screen

01:01:57.672 --> 01:01:59.298
and the chassis of the phone,

01:01:59.298 --> 01:02:02.051
which, out of the factory, is designed

01:02:02.051 --> 01:02:05.430
so that it's sealed away from
elements like dust and dirt.

01:02:15.022 --> 01:02:17.150
Ear-speaker cleaning is one of the things

01:02:17.150 --> 01:02:18.067
what we get all the time

01:02:18.067 --> 01:02:20.069
where somebody's phone's really quiet.

01:02:21.237 --> 01:02:23.948
10 to 20% of the phones what we get in

01:02:23.948 --> 01:02:25.450
actually need cleaning out.

01:02:26.701 --> 01:02:29.829
The easiest way to clean
out an iPhone ear speaker

01:02:29.829 --> 01:02:32.832
is using some isopropyl alcohol.

01:02:32.832 --> 01:02:35.835
The customer will ask for
a ear-speaker replacing,

01:02:35.835 --> 01:02:38.171
but in reality, it just
needs cleaning out.

01:02:38.171 --> 01:02:40.798
And even if we did
replace the ear speaker,

01:02:40.798 --> 01:02:42.341
it probably would still be quiet

01:02:42.341 --> 01:02:45.178
because that mesh is just
bunged up and gunked up.

01:02:45.178 --> 01:02:48.556
Then we loosen up the
gunk with a toothbrush.

01:02:48.556 --> 01:02:49.932
Make sure that it's not the toothbrush

01:02:49.932 --> 01:02:52.518
that you've used this
morning to brush your teeth.

01:02:52.518 --> 01:02:54.395
And then we play this sound,

01:02:54.395 --> 01:02:57.899
which plays a 165-hertz sound,

01:02:57.899 --> 01:02:59.442
[sound humming]
which acts as a sort of

01:02:59.442 --> 01:03:02.153
[sound humming]
ultrasonic cleaner.

01:03:02.153 --> 01:03:05.198
For the most part, I think
that the most common thing

01:03:05.198 --> 01:03:07.784
that gets in ear speaker
is probably earwax.

01:03:10.745 --> 01:03:13.664
I don't really want to think
about what else it could be,

01:03:13.664 --> 01:03:17.418
what's getting in there,
because it's, yeah, it's gross.

01:03:34.268 --> 01:03:36.646
We use two different types of brushes.

01:03:36.646 --> 01:03:39.482
For the softer brush we use a toothbrush,

01:03:39.482 --> 01:03:41.442
and then a slightly more stiff one

01:03:41.442 --> 01:03:43.736
is this small horsehair brush.

01:03:44.695 --> 01:03:46.614
The vibrations act as an agitator,

01:03:46.614 --> 01:03:48.115
and it loosens the dirt,

01:03:48.115 --> 01:03:50.743
what's in those tiny little
gaps in the ear speaker.

01:03:54.956 --> 01:03:57.083
So, the cloth over the speaker,

01:03:57.083 --> 01:03:59.001
we add a little bit of alcohol onto there.

01:03:59.001 --> 01:04:00.419
And the way that that works

01:04:00.419 --> 01:04:05.007
is just adding a finer
layer of cleaning stuff

01:04:05.007 --> 01:04:08.219
just to get any remaining
bits of dust out of there.

01:04:18.980 --> 01:04:21.399
So, for this one, it's a iPhone 11 Pro,

01:04:21.399 --> 01:04:24.652
and it's got a little bit of
gunk in the speaker grille

01:04:24.652 --> 01:04:27.655
at the bottom, which would
make the phone pretty quiet.

01:04:27.655 --> 01:04:29.198
This is very similar

01:04:29.198 --> 01:04:31.576
to what gets built up
inside the charge port.

01:04:31.576 --> 01:04:32.743
It's just pocket gunk.

01:04:32.743 --> 01:04:34.287
The best tool for this one

01:04:34.287 --> 01:04:38.082
is the sharp, pointed BGA scraper again.

01:04:42.545 --> 01:04:45.256
The debris in these holes
will often look like

01:04:45.256 --> 01:04:48.134
a browny, sort of beige color,

01:04:48.134 --> 01:04:49.927
whereas the actual speakers are black,

01:04:49.927 --> 01:04:52.221
so you'll know that
you've got it in there.

01:05:47.526 --> 01:05:49.195
I sort of got involved

01:05:49.195 --> 01:05:51.072
with fixing friends' and family's phones,

01:05:51.072 --> 01:05:53.282
sorting out technical problems for them,

01:05:53.282 --> 01:05:56.285
and once I started learning
about phone repair,

01:05:56.285 --> 01:05:57.870
I just got deeper and deeper into it.

01:05:57.870 --> 01:05:58.746
It's like a rabbit hole,

01:05:58.746 --> 01:06:00.831
and I just got fully sucked down it.

01:06:02.667 --> 01:06:06.879
We receive about 20 iPhones
per week in the post,

01:06:06.879 --> 01:06:09.924
and we probably take in

01:06:09.924 --> 01:06:13.803
from walk-in customers between
five and 15 phones a day.

01:06:16.472 --> 01:06:18.265
You get lots of instances where somebody

01:06:18.265 --> 01:06:20.601
might bring a phone in, and
it might be a minor thing,

01:06:20.601 --> 01:06:22.979
and they say, "Oh, look, my
phone's completely dead."

01:06:22.979 --> 01:06:25.731
And it's really good to
see people very happy

01:06:25.731 --> 01:06:27.400
once you've repaired their devices.

01:06:34.031 --> 01:06:35.116
Narrator: Back in the US, 

01:06:35.157 --> 01:06:38.077
Trae Zipperer shows us how to properly clean

01:06:38.077 --> 01:06:41.831
forgotten veteran's gravestones
covered with moss and algae.

01:06:45.292 --> 01:06:47.753
So, I get a lot of questions about,

01:06:47.753 --> 01:06:49.755
"Why are these stones so dirty?"

01:06:49.755 --> 01:06:52.675
This is not dirt. These
are living organisms.

01:06:52.675 --> 01:06:54.176
These are plants.

01:06:54.176 --> 01:06:57.596
The more polished the stone,
the less surface area there is

01:06:57.596 --> 01:07:02.184
for the biological organisms
to adhere to the stone.

01:07:02.184 --> 01:07:03.602
And so the reason why we use

01:07:03.602 --> 01:07:07.023
this quaternary ammonium
compound is this kills them.

01:07:07.023 --> 01:07:08.899
So, these things are natural in nature.

01:07:08.899 --> 01:07:11.235
You're going to find in
the South especially,

01:07:11.235 --> 01:07:13.237
it's not just growing on rooftops,

01:07:13.237 --> 01:07:16.240
it's also growing on our headstones.

01:07:19.201 --> 01:07:21.162
My name is Trae Zipperer.

01:07:21.162 --> 01:07:23.330
I'm the founder of By Memorial Day,

01:07:23.330 --> 01:07:26.167
a nonprofit organization
dedicated to the cleaning

01:07:26.167 --> 01:07:29.962
of all veteran headstones by
Memorial Day in perpetuity.

01:07:32.506 --> 01:07:35.342
So, this is a ground-level grave marker

01:07:35.342 --> 01:07:36.927
issued by the federal government

01:07:36.927 --> 01:07:39.096
via the National Cemetery Administration.

01:07:39.096 --> 01:07:42.266
It's a white marble
material, natural stone.

01:07:42.266 --> 01:07:47.021
He was a gunner's mate,
GM, third class, US Navy.

01:07:47.021 --> 01:07:49.398
I don't think it's respectful to have

01:07:49.398 --> 01:07:52.443
a veteran's headstone dirty in the least.

01:07:52.443 --> 01:07:55.112
And so we're going to clean this headstone

01:07:55.112 --> 01:07:57.323
of this American hero who gave his all,

01:07:57.323 --> 01:08:00.326
who sacrificed unimaginable horrors

01:08:00.326 --> 01:08:02.870
for three and a half years
as a prisoner of war.

01:08:02.870 --> 01:08:04.830
The first thing I like to do is

01:08:04.830 --> 01:08:08.000
I use a desk broom

01:08:08.000 --> 01:08:12.505
to get all of the sand and
debris off of the stone.

01:08:12.505 --> 01:08:15.299
So, we take our water-squirt bottle.

01:08:15.299 --> 01:08:18.094
I like the one with a little
bit bigger base on it.

01:08:18.094 --> 01:08:20.721
I give it a coating of water,

01:08:20.721 --> 01:08:23.182
because, again, these stones are porous,

01:08:23.182 --> 01:08:25.976
so it's going to soak in this clean water,

01:08:25.976 --> 01:08:27.603
which is not going to get

01:08:27.603 --> 01:08:30.356
any chemical down inside the stone.

01:08:30.356 --> 01:08:33.984
We use a cleaning agent that
contains an active ingredient

01:08:33.984 --> 01:08:37.113
known as a quaternary ammonium compound.

01:08:37.113 --> 01:08:38.948
And it is known and respected

01:08:38.948 --> 01:08:42.701
throughout historic
preservationists as the safest,

01:08:42.701 --> 01:08:45.871
most effective way to clean natural stone.

01:08:45.871 --> 01:08:50.793
This brush is made with
bristles from Tampico.

01:08:50.793 --> 01:08:54.505
So the Tampico from the agave
plant is a natural bristle

01:08:54.505 --> 01:08:56.507
that won't harm the natural stone.

01:08:56.507 --> 01:08:58.259
And then I want to give it a good coating

01:08:58.259 --> 01:09:00.803
of the D/2 Biological Solution,

01:09:00.803 --> 01:09:04.181
which is going to go to work
on this stone very quickly.

01:09:04.181 --> 01:09:06.058
You're going to see this stone

01:09:06.058 --> 01:09:09.186
starting to turn a bit of an orange color.

01:09:09.186 --> 01:09:11.730
And then it's going to
continue working on the stone

01:09:11.730 --> 01:09:13.399
for the next few weeks,

01:09:13.399 --> 01:09:17.319
and that stone will become
brighter and whiter and whiter.

01:09:17.319 --> 01:09:20.447
And you have to clean them
about every two to three years.

01:09:20.447 --> 01:09:22.241
This does a wonderful job

01:09:22.241 --> 01:09:25.953
of getting all the growth
killed on the stone.

01:09:30.416 --> 01:09:34.670
The next step is to let that
soak for about 10 minutes.

01:09:34.670 --> 01:09:37.047
When I'm looking at a
headstone in a cemetery,

01:09:37.047 --> 01:09:40.134
I want that stone to be impeccably clean.

01:09:40.134 --> 01:09:42.803
It doesn't matter if it's almost clean.

01:09:42.803 --> 01:09:44.388
It needs to be clean.

01:09:44.388 --> 01:09:47.099
I think the reason why
some veteran headstones

01:09:47.099 --> 01:09:51.896
become more soiled or more
covered in biological growth

01:09:51.896 --> 01:09:54.481
is because of the polishing of the stone.

01:09:54.481 --> 01:09:57.985
The more polished the stone,
the less surface area there is

01:09:57.985 --> 01:10:02.823
for the biological organisms
to adhere to the stone.

01:10:02.823 --> 01:10:04.074
And after about 10 minutes,

01:10:04.074 --> 01:10:06.869
it's not that hard to get
the material off the stone

01:10:06.869 --> 01:10:10.623
with a soft-bristle,
natural-bristle brush.

01:10:29.516 --> 01:10:32.186
Then you can get into the
etchings with a toothbrush

01:10:32.186 --> 01:10:35.606
to get into the real nooks and
crannies of things sometimes.

01:10:49.954 --> 01:10:53.582
And the final step of
honoring a veteran of our past

01:10:53.582 --> 01:10:56.085
is to place an American
flag at their grave.

01:10:56.085 --> 01:10:58.087
This is known as a grave flag.

01:10:58.087 --> 01:11:00.464
This particular grave has already had

01:11:00.464 --> 01:11:04.385
an American Legion flag
holder placed at the grave,

01:11:04.385 --> 01:11:06.262
and there's a spot in the back of them

01:11:06.262 --> 01:11:09.723
for the flag stick to just
stick right down into it.

01:11:12.351 --> 01:11:14.520
My vision of By Memorial Day is that

01:11:14.520 --> 01:11:17.106
one day in the near future,
we'll have a volunteer

01:11:17.106 --> 01:11:19.984
assigned to every
veteran's grave in America,

01:11:19.984 --> 01:11:21.777
no matter where that veteran is buried,

01:11:21.777 --> 01:11:23.529
no matter what cemetery they're in.

01:11:25.239 --> 01:11:28.158
So, to tell you a little
bit about why this happens,

01:11:28.158 --> 01:11:29.910
these are living organisms.

01:11:29.910 --> 01:11:32.997
These are plants growing on the stone,

01:11:32.997 --> 01:11:36.083
whether they're algae or lichens

01:11:36.083 --> 01:11:39.545
or mold, mildew.

01:11:39.545 --> 01:11:41.130
And so the reason why we use this

01:11:41.130 --> 01:11:44.341
quaternary ammonium
compound is this kills them.

01:11:44.341 --> 01:11:46.176
So these things are natural in nature.

01:11:46.176 --> 01:11:48.721
You're going to find in
the South especially,

01:11:48.721 --> 01:11:52.141
we have this black mold on all of our

01:11:52.141 --> 01:11:54.393
concrete-tile roofs.

01:11:54.393 --> 01:11:56.937
And it's not just growing on rooftops;

01:11:56.937 --> 01:12:00.107
it's also growing on our headstones.

01:12:08.365 --> 01:12:11.368
I think it's important that
we don't lose the stories

01:12:11.368 --> 01:12:13.495
of our veteran heroes of the past,

01:12:13.495 --> 01:12:16.665
because they truly are forgotten.

01:12:16.665 --> 01:12:20.669
Their stories are disappearing
because when they pass away

01:12:20.669 --> 01:12:23.130
and the people who knew them pass away,

01:12:23.130 --> 01:12:25.883
their stories are pretty
much gone forever.

01:12:31.221 --> 01:12:33.807
So, now we're going to
start on the etchings.

01:12:33.807 --> 01:12:35.392
I start with the cross at the top

01:12:35.392 --> 01:12:38.187
and work my way down just each letter.

01:12:38.187 --> 01:12:42.024
These are etched into the marble.

01:12:42.024 --> 01:12:45.694
As compared to the
early veteran headstones

01:12:45.694 --> 01:12:47.946
known as bas-relief, where the letters

01:12:47.946 --> 01:12:49.948
were basically sticking out,

01:12:49.948 --> 01:12:52.326
they removed the material
around the letters.

01:12:52.326 --> 01:12:55.287
So the letters became
kind of three-dimensional.

01:12:55.287 --> 01:12:58.082
The material that grows
inside these stones,

01:12:58.082 --> 01:13:01.293
it's a combination of
things fixed in there,

01:13:01.293 --> 01:13:03.670
and also there's little
ledges, so to speak,

01:13:03.670 --> 01:13:06.298
where when the wind
blows, the pollen falls,

01:13:06.298 --> 01:13:09.426
all different types of
things will just get wedged

01:13:09.426 --> 01:13:11.845
and just build up in these etchings.

01:13:19.228 --> 01:13:22.439
Grave flags measure 8 inches by 12 inches,

01:13:22.439 --> 01:13:25.901
but I would rather have
a flag than no flag,

01:13:25.901 --> 01:13:27.820
so if you can't find an 8-by-12,

01:13:27.820 --> 01:13:30.572
that doesn't mean you can't
place a flag at the grave.

01:13:30.572 --> 01:13:33.033
The person at a podium
speaking, for example,

01:13:33.033 --> 01:13:34.785
the American flag is to their right.

01:13:34.785 --> 01:13:38.580
So in this case, we have a
headstone of an American veteran.

01:13:38.580 --> 01:13:40.082
So as the veteran's looking at you,

01:13:40.082 --> 01:13:42.209
the flag needs to be to his or her right.

01:13:42.209 --> 01:13:44.336
You want to get as close
to the stone as you can

01:13:44.336 --> 01:13:46.713
without marring the
stone with your mallet.

01:13:46.713 --> 01:13:48.215
Because when the weed eaters

01:13:48.215 --> 01:13:51.176
and other types of maintenance
equipment comes through,

01:13:51.176 --> 01:13:53.220
you don't want them
hitting your flag holder.

01:13:56.473 --> 01:13:58.225
The way that I research the veterans

01:13:58.225 --> 01:14:01.311
buried beneath the veteran
headstones that I clean

01:14:01.311 --> 01:14:03.605
is by using the internet first.

01:14:03.605 --> 01:14:07.526
And I go to certain sources
that I know contain content,

01:14:07.526 --> 01:14:10.320
such as findagrave.com.

01:14:10.320 --> 01:14:13.198
There's several different
websites like that.

01:14:13.198 --> 01:14:16.452
And by looking into
those different databases

01:14:16.452 --> 01:14:19.913
and different family
trees on ancestry.com,

01:14:19.913 --> 01:14:22.833
you can start to put together
the story of a veteran.

01:14:22.833 --> 01:14:26.462
And every now and then you just
get something that's amazing

01:14:26.462 --> 01:14:28.922
and insightful that you didn't anticipate.

01:14:30.090 --> 01:14:33.844
This is a application
for a headstone or marker

01:14:33.844 --> 01:14:37.848
for a deceased American veteran.

01:14:37.848 --> 01:14:40.350
The family members would have
to complete this application

01:14:40.350 --> 01:14:43.145
in order to choose what
style of veteran headstone

01:14:43.145 --> 01:14:44.771
that they wanted for their loved one.

01:14:44.771 --> 01:14:47.691
And this particular application is for

01:14:47.691 --> 01:14:50.277
Robert S. Lewis,

01:14:50.277 --> 01:14:51.862
prepared by his father.

01:14:51.862 --> 01:14:55.532
And this is where the
story of his sacrifice

01:14:55.532 --> 01:14:56.867
became apparent.

01:14:59.578 --> 01:15:01.079
This man was a sergeant.

01:15:01.079 --> 01:15:04.416
Sgt. Shadman was a top turret gunner.

01:15:04.416 --> 01:15:07.377
"Fire in the cockpit. Could
not see if he got out.

01:15:07.377 --> 01:15:10.214
I saw Sgt. Shadman
reaching for a parachute.

01:15:10.214 --> 01:15:12.341
I do not believe he could
have gotten out alive

01:15:12.341 --> 01:15:15.177
because the plane broke apart
where he was last seen."

01:15:15.177 --> 01:15:18.388
When they were attacked, they
were flying at 24,000 feet.

01:15:18.388 --> 01:15:22.017
Sgt. Shadman was up in the
top turret, he was a gunner,

01:15:22.017 --> 01:15:23.894
and he was trying to get
out of that top turret

01:15:23.894 --> 01:15:25.938
as the plane was tumbling to the ground.

01:15:25.938 --> 01:15:29.650
His death's date says September 12, 1944.

01:15:29.650 --> 01:15:32.486
But there's evidence to
suggest that he did in fact

01:15:32.486 --> 01:15:35.739
get out of that aircraft and
he did reach the ground alive

01:15:35.739 --> 01:15:38.367
because there's a hospital
admission record of him

01:15:38.367 --> 01:15:40.285
in September 1944.

01:15:40.285 --> 01:15:41.912
We don't know what day that he died,

01:15:41.912 --> 01:15:45.499
but his mother applied
for his veteran headstone

01:15:45.499 --> 01:15:47.876
with a death date of September 23rd.

01:15:49.211 --> 01:15:52.631
In your local cemeteries,
there are local heroes

01:15:52.631 --> 01:15:55.926
whose graves are in really bad condition,

01:15:55.926 --> 01:15:58.095
and it's pretty sad that a man or a woman

01:15:58.095 --> 01:15:59.846
gives their life for our country

01:15:59.846 --> 01:16:01.557
and then they're forgotten in the cemetery

01:16:01.557 --> 01:16:03.392
and their headstone can't even be read

01:16:03.392 --> 01:16:06.061
because there's so much
biological filth on it.

01:16:06.061 --> 01:16:09.898
There are people in America who
care about veterans' graves,

01:16:09.898 --> 01:16:12.109
they just don't know
what's happening out here.

01:16:12.109 --> 01:16:14.278
So I want to bring this story to them

01:16:14.278 --> 01:16:16.530
so that they know that
in your local cemeteries,

01:16:16.530 --> 01:16:19.408
there are local heroes whose graves

01:16:19.408 --> 01:16:21.660
are in really bad condition.

01:16:21.660 --> 01:16:24.121
So if this is something
that speaks to your heart,

01:16:24.121 --> 01:16:26.540
I encourage you to get out
in your local cemetery,

01:16:26.540 --> 01:16:28.750
see for yourself if
there's veteran headstones

01:16:28.750 --> 01:16:31.253
in need of cleaning in your local area,

01:16:31.253 --> 01:16:34.673
and take the initiative
to do something about it.

01:16:42.639 --> 01:16:45.934
Narrator: This is a rare
Persian rug that's appraised

01:16:45.934 --> 01:16:49.521
at an estimated $12,500,

01:16:49.521 --> 01:16:51.773
and it's about to get its first cleaning

01:16:51.773 --> 01:16:53.817
in more than two decades.

01:16:53.817 --> 01:16:55.360
It's a delicate job,

01:16:55.360 --> 01:16:58.238
and not just because this
antique is so valuable.

01:16:58.238 --> 01:17:00.365
It's also a family heirloom,

01:17:00.365 --> 01:17:03.243
and the clients are trusting
the team at Rouzati Rugs

01:17:03.243 --> 01:17:05.203
to deep clean it carefully.

01:17:05.203 --> 01:17:07.581
The goal is to remove the accumulation

01:17:07.581 --> 01:17:10.751
of dirt and stains without
harming the rug's coloring.

01:17:10.751 --> 01:17:13.837
Ali Rouzati inspects the fibers carefully

01:17:13.837 --> 01:17:16.423
to determine how the
rug was originally dyed.

01:17:16.423 --> 01:17:19.760
This key detail will determine
how he'll complete the job

01:17:19.760 --> 01:17:21.511
without causing any damage,

01:17:21.511 --> 01:17:23.722
so his clients can continue to pass down

01:17:23.722 --> 01:17:25.932
this treasure for generations.

01:17:25.932 --> 01:17:29.269
Ali: So, my client's aunt had
this rug for about 20 years,

01:17:29.269 --> 01:17:31.229
and she was very hesitant
to have it cleaned

01:17:31.229 --> 01:17:33.231
because she was afraid
that it would get ruined.

01:17:33.231 --> 01:17:35.525
I know this is a 1940s rug

01:17:35.525 --> 01:17:37.778
because they used vegetable dyes,

01:17:37.778 --> 01:17:42.491
in comparison to a 1960s
Tabriz, like this one here.

01:17:42.491 --> 01:17:46.036
There's a specific process to
cleaning vegetable-dye rugs.

01:17:46.036 --> 01:17:47.704
I know that I can use a deep wash

01:17:47.704 --> 01:17:49.247
because the dyes won't run.

01:17:49.247 --> 01:17:53.710
This is the pool that we
have the rugs cleaned,

01:17:53.710 --> 01:17:57.339
and we have a drain right here.

01:17:57.339 --> 01:17:58.632
It's built on an angle

01:17:58.632 --> 01:18:01.134
so that when we put a rug on either side,

01:18:01.134 --> 01:18:03.261
the water will just go right through

01:18:03.261 --> 01:18:05.013
directly into the drain.

01:18:06.515 --> 01:18:08.058
Narrator: One of Ali's team members

01:18:08.058 --> 01:18:09.559
begins the cleaning process

01:18:09.559 --> 01:18:12.396
by applying browning
treatment to the stains.

01:18:12.396 --> 01:18:14.773
This works to remove tannin-type stains

01:18:14.773 --> 01:18:18.068
like urine, fruit juice, beer, or soil

01:18:18.068 --> 01:18:20.862
as well as general
discoloration due to age.

01:18:22.155 --> 01:18:25.867
This 1940s Sheikh Mashayekhi
rug has collected stains

01:18:25.867 --> 01:18:28.453
from various family gatherings
throughout the years,

01:18:28.453 --> 01:18:31.331
but that's not all that
makes this Tabriz rug unique.

01:18:31.331 --> 01:18:34.251
What makes it so special is

01:18:34.251 --> 01:18:36.420
not only the technique of weave

01:18:36.420 --> 01:18:38.588
and the fine weaving on the back,

01:18:38.588 --> 01:18:40.215
but also the design

01:18:40.215 --> 01:18:42.759
and the color combinations
that are used as well.

01:18:42.759 --> 01:18:44.177
Narrator: After letting
the browning treatment

01:18:44.177 --> 01:18:45.762
sit for 15 minutes,

01:18:45.762 --> 01:18:48.724
he hoses down the rug and applies shampoo.

01:18:54.980 --> 01:18:58.108
Next, he agitates the
fibers with a scrubber,

01:18:58.108 --> 01:19:00.485
which loosens up the accumulated dirt

01:19:00.485 --> 01:19:02.529
and brings it to the surface.

01:19:02.529 --> 01:19:05.115
Ali: So, the scrubber or the buffer

01:19:05.115 --> 01:19:08.744
is used to help scrub

01:19:08.744 --> 01:19:13.123
and expand the use of the
shampoo throughout the rug,

01:19:13.123 --> 01:19:15.709
and it does it in a
fast and efficient way.

01:19:15.709 --> 01:19:17.711
So if you were to actually hand-do this,

01:19:17.711 --> 01:19:19.755
not only would you get tired physically,

01:19:19.755 --> 01:19:21.465
but it wouldn't be as effective.

01:19:23.091 --> 01:19:25.135
Narrator: Ali estimates
that the original weaver

01:19:25.135 --> 01:19:28.638
made only a few hundred
of these rugs decades ago.

01:19:28.638 --> 01:19:31.266
The coloring also dates back to a time

01:19:31.266 --> 01:19:34.311
when master weavers used
different techniques.

01:19:34.311 --> 01:19:37.272
This rug is made with vegetable-dyed wool,

01:19:37.272 --> 01:19:40.275
which means the coloring
came from natural materials

01:19:40.275 --> 01:19:43.111
like plants, fruits, or tree bark.

01:19:43.111 --> 01:19:45.489
This method is more labor-intensive,

01:19:45.489 --> 01:19:47.908
which adds to the rug's value.

01:19:47.908 --> 01:19:50.035
Ali: Chrome dyes come from chemicals

01:19:50.035 --> 01:19:53.705
that are used and boiled
to create certain colors.

01:19:53.705 --> 01:19:57.584
Chrome dyes were used to decrease the cost

01:19:57.584 --> 01:19:59.836
of rug weaving.

01:19:59.836 --> 01:20:02.422
So in terms of price point,

01:20:02.422 --> 01:20:04.466
they are less in comparison to rugs

01:20:04.466 --> 01:20:07.260
that have vegetable dyes
incorporated in them.

01:20:07.260 --> 01:20:10.972
As you can see, this type
of a red tone is so bright.

01:20:10.972 --> 01:20:13.016
You only see that in chrome dyes.

01:20:13.016 --> 01:20:14.142
But with vegetable dyes,

01:20:14.142 --> 01:20:16.520
they're a little bit more
subtle in comparison.

01:20:17.604 --> 01:20:19.397
Narrator: The transition
from vegetable dyes

01:20:19.397 --> 01:20:21.858
to chrome dyes in the mid-20th century

01:20:21.858 --> 01:20:24.569
brought about a new era in rug making.

01:20:24.569 --> 01:20:26.446
Chrome dyes offered a wider

01:20:26.446 --> 01:20:28.615
and more affordable range of hues,

01:20:28.615 --> 01:20:30.742
which revolutionized the industry.

01:20:30.742 --> 01:20:33.370
But the legacy of those
early Persian rug makers

01:20:33.370 --> 01:20:36.498
is still visible today in rugs like this,

01:20:36.498 --> 01:20:40.126
which is why it's so
important to preserve it.

01:20:40.126 --> 01:20:43.296
The team member rinses the
rug and pushes a squeegee

01:20:43.296 --> 01:20:46.424
across each section to
remove excess water.

01:20:46.424 --> 01:20:48.260
He repeats this step several times

01:20:48.260 --> 01:20:49.636
throughout the cleaning process

01:20:49.636 --> 01:20:52.514
to remove as much water as possible.

01:20:53.765 --> 01:20:56.184
He runs the scrubber across the rug again

01:20:56.184 --> 01:20:58.061
to loosen any remaining dirt

01:20:58.061 --> 01:21:00.856
and then uses the extractor
to lift the stains

01:21:00.856 --> 01:21:03.066
and remove moisture.

01:21:03.066 --> 01:21:05.819
Ali: The extractor
actually has two purposes.

01:21:05.819 --> 01:21:08.071
You can, one, just use it as a vacuum.

01:21:08.071 --> 01:21:10.073
You just place it on the rug,

01:21:10.073 --> 01:21:12.075
and you start pulling it
towards your direction,

01:21:12.075 --> 01:21:14.286
and it starts extracting the water out

01:21:14.286 --> 01:21:16.705
so that the rug gets to dry faster.

01:21:16.705 --> 01:21:18.874
Also, it has another feature

01:21:18.874 --> 01:21:20.750
where if you press on the lever,

01:21:20.750 --> 01:21:23.003
not only will it extract water,

01:21:23.003 --> 01:21:25.088
but it'll expel hot
water at the same time.

01:21:25.088 --> 01:21:27.090
And those are great for taking away stains

01:21:27.090 --> 01:21:30.719
because you could do that
multiple times on a certain area.

01:21:32.262 --> 01:21:34.931
All the rugs are heated
to a high temperature

01:21:34.931 --> 01:21:37.434
of I'd say about 90 degrees on average,

01:21:37.434 --> 01:21:39.644
and they're hung right here.

01:21:39.644 --> 01:21:41.479
Narrator: The rug must be completely dry

01:21:41.479 --> 01:21:43.523
before moving on to the next step.

01:21:43.523 --> 01:21:47.527
If not, the excess moisture
can lead to mold and mildew,

01:21:47.527 --> 01:21:51.615
two factors that can deteriorate
the fibers in the rug.

01:21:51.615 --> 01:21:53.491
Ali: We'll go inside of the drying room,

01:21:53.491 --> 01:21:56.161
and we'll examine it and
touch the pile of the rug,

01:21:56.161 --> 01:21:58.580
the fringes, and the
other aspects of the rug

01:21:58.580 --> 01:22:00.457
to see if it's still moist or not.

01:22:00.457 --> 01:22:02.375
If we feel it is, we'll keep it in there.

01:22:02.375 --> 01:22:06.129
The room is dry to a
very high temperature,

01:22:06.129 --> 01:22:08.757
and just having all the
fans and dehumidifiers,

01:22:08.757 --> 01:22:11.134
usually having a rug in
there for about a day

01:22:11.134 --> 01:22:12.761
would completely dry it out.

01:22:14.179 --> 01:22:16.723
Narrator: The quality
of weave on Tabriz rugs

01:22:16.723 --> 01:22:19.100
is based on something called raj,

01:22:19.100 --> 01:22:22.979
which counts the number of
linear knots every 7 centimeters,

01:22:22.979 --> 01:22:25.440
which is roughly 2 ½ inches.

01:22:25.440 --> 01:22:27.817
The higher the raj,
the higher the quality,

01:22:27.817 --> 01:22:29.861
which also makes a rug more valuable.

01:22:29.861 --> 01:22:31.363
So, in today's market,

01:22:31.363 --> 01:22:35.659
I would estimate this
value around $12,500.

01:22:35.659 --> 01:22:37.452
You have these lines that are showing

01:22:37.452 --> 01:22:39.496
on the backside of the rug,

01:22:39.496 --> 01:22:41.706
and when you look at those lines,

01:22:41.706 --> 01:22:43.124
you'll be able to actually count

01:22:43.124 --> 01:22:46.753
how many knots are in between each line.

01:22:46.753 --> 01:22:50.465
And so this specific rug is 50 raj.

01:22:50.465 --> 01:22:54.177
On a lower-quality Tabriz,
you'll have a 20 raj.

01:22:54.177 --> 01:22:56.012
This is actually a lower-quality Tabriz.

01:22:56.012 --> 01:22:59.057
You can see how big the knots are

01:22:59.057 --> 01:23:01.643
in comparison to this rug here.

01:23:01.643 --> 01:23:03.853
These knots are a lot more tight.

01:23:03.853 --> 01:23:07.732
This is around that 20 raj
that I had mentioned earlier.

01:23:07.732 --> 01:23:09.609
And then you have the 50 raj,

01:23:09.609 --> 01:23:12.320
which is a more higher quality.

01:23:12.320 --> 01:23:15.573
60 raj would be very exceptional.

01:23:15.573 --> 01:23:18.076
And then you'll have 80 or 90 raj,

01:23:18.076 --> 01:23:20.036
which are extremely rare to find,

01:23:20.036 --> 01:23:23.415
and those type of rugs take
close to 10 years to make.

01:23:23.415 --> 01:23:25.792
But this type of a caliber,

01:23:25.792 --> 01:23:29.504
I'd say in terms of rarity,
on a scale of one to 10,

01:23:29.504 --> 01:23:30.672
it would be a seven.

01:23:30.672 --> 01:23:32.716
So, when you look at the
different color combinations

01:23:32.716 --> 01:23:35.343
and designs that are used in this rug,

01:23:35.343 --> 01:23:38.763
it seems like there's a lot of
religious symbolism going on,

01:23:38.763 --> 01:23:41.474
and that makes sense
because Sheikh Mashayekhi

01:23:41.474 --> 01:23:44.644
was a religious man within his region.

01:23:44.644 --> 01:23:46.271
And the predominant use of green

01:23:46.271 --> 01:23:48.690
is of Islamic religious significance

01:23:48.690 --> 01:23:52.152
because green was the prophet
Muhammad's favorite color.

01:23:52.152 --> 01:23:54.821
I've grown up with
Persian rugs in my home,

01:23:54.821 --> 01:23:58.074
and there's a lot of deep
cultural significance

01:23:58.074 --> 01:24:00.994
to Persian rugs and the history behind it.

01:24:00.994 --> 01:24:04.664
So I feel very akin to my culture

01:24:04.664 --> 01:24:06.124
being in this industry.

01:24:11.463 --> 01:24:13.006
The rug looks magnificent.

01:24:13.006 --> 01:24:15.216
It's much brighter than it was before,

01:24:15.216 --> 01:24:18.428
and the majority of the
stains have been removed.

01:24:18.428 --> 01:24:21.514
There are a lot of other
people in this industry

01:24:21.514 --> 01:24:24.142
that really don't put their heart into it.

01:24:24.142 --> 01:24:26.478
They just clock in and clock out.

01:24:26.478 --> 01:24:29.522
But each rug is

01:24:29.522 --> 01:24:33.234
a portion of a person's home,

01:24:33.234 --> 01:24:36.696
and I take that responsibility to heart,

01:24:36.696 --> 01:24:38.656
that when they bring their rug to me

01:24:38.656 --> 01:24:43.286
they're bringing a part
of their home into my life

01:24:43.286 --> 01:24:45.121
for me to take care of.

